window.WL_TEXT={"008":{"iso":"008","name":"Albania","coverage":null,"summary":"Albania runs one national school system: nine untracked years, then a State Matura exam ends upper secondary.","different":["Unlike the U.S.'s 13,000 local districts, Albania's Ministry of Education and Sport sets curriculum and policy for general public schools; vocational high schools fall under the Ministry of Finance.","Unlike the optional U.S. SAT/ACT, all Albanian upper-secondary students must pass the State Matura Exam to finish school.","Albania keeps students in one untracked basic-education stream to age 16, then splits them into gymnasium, vocational, or arts/sports schools, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools."],"common":["Like the U.S., Albania has both public and private schools operating at every level of education."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"16","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education and Sport)","exit_exam":"State Matura Exam"},"sources":[{"title":"Albania - Overview - Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/albania/overview"},{"title":"Albania - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/encyclopedia/albania/"}]},"031":{"iso":"031","name":"Azerbaijan","coverage":null,"summary":"Azerbaijan centralizes curriculum and exams nationally, and sorts students at 15 into general or vocational secondary school.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Azerbaijan's Ministry of Science and Education sets national curriculum standards and a single education policy for every school.","After grade 9 (age 15), Azerbaijani students choose upper-secondary general school or vocational education, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools."],"common":["Like the U.S., Azerbaijan has both public (free, state-run) and private (tuition-charging) schools operating side by side."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15 (after grade 9)","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Science and Education sets state standards and curricula)","exit_exam":"State final attestation (Certificate of General Secondary Education), after grade 11"},"sources":[{"title":"Azerbaijan (TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia)","url":"https://timss2023.org/encyclopedia/azerbaijan/"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"096":{"iso":"096","name":"Brunei","coverage":null,"summary":"Brunei's Ministry of Education runs one national system (SPN21); most secondary students end in the British-style Cambridge O-Level exam.","different":["Unlike U.S. state and local control, Brunei's national Ministry of Education runs one system (SPN21) covering nearly all schools' structure and curriculum.","At the end of Year 8 (about 14), an exam channels Brunei students into separate General, Applied, or other secondary programmes; U.S. high schools don't sort students this way.","Brunei's Cambridge O-Level and IGCSE exams end General or Applied secondary school (Year 10 or 11); the U.S. has no national school-leaving exam."],"common":[],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"14 (end of Year 8, Student Progress Examination)","curriculum":"National (SPN21 framework, Ministry of Education)","exit_exam":"Brunei-Cambridge GCE O-Level and IGCSE, taken at the end of General or Applied secondary education (Year 10 or 11 depending on track)"},"sources":[{"title":"Secondary Education - Ministry of Education, Brunei Darussalam","url":"https://www.moe.gov.bn/secondary-education/"},{"title":"Primary Education - Ministry of Education, Brunei Darussalam","url":"https://www.moe.gov.bn/primary-education/"},{"title":"National Education System - SEAMEO Brunei Darussalam","url":"https://www.seameo.org/Main_about/93"}],"_audit":{"supported":4,"corrected":4,"deleted":1}},"156":{"iso":"156","name":"China (Beijing, Shanghai, Jiangsu, Zhejiang)","coverage":"Scores cover only the provinces/municipalities of Beijing, Shanghai, Jiangsu and Zhejiang, not all of China.","v2":{"rows":{"curriculum":{"text":"The Ministry of Education sets national curriculum standards and approves textbooks. Shanghai may have its own curriculum within that framework, and its schools can design 30% as school-based curriculum (2016).","cites":[{"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf","quote":"the Ministry of Education sets overall national curriculum standards for all subjects. Shanghai, along with a few selected provinces, is allowed to have its own curriculum and has released its subject-specific \"Curriculum Plan for Regular Elementary and Secondary Schools in Shanghai.\" Shanghai schools further have the autonomy to design 30 percent of the curriculum as school-based curriculum"},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/shanghai-china/","quote":"It is responsible for setting the national curriculum, establishing standard syllabi for required subjects, evaluating and approving textbooks and teaching materials"},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/shanghai-china/","quote":"Shanghai has moved aggressively to reform its curriculum within the limits of the national framework."}]},"sorting":{"text":"Shanghai students attend their neighborhood school through lower secondary, not selective 'key schools'. After ninth grade, exam scores decide placement; roughly half of low scorers enter vocational schools.","cites":[{"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf","quote":"The Chinese system of education had a long tradition of key schools, or public schools that received greater resources than other schools and were more selective. Shanghai requires its students to attend their local neighborhood school at the primary and lower secondary levels rather than compete for limited spots at key schools."},{"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf","quote":"At the end of ninth grade, students take the written senior secondary school entrance examination in the subjects of Chinese, math, English, physics, and chemistry."},{"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf","quote":"The total score on the examination determines the type of senior secondary school in which students will enroll: admission into highly selective model senior secondary schools requires outstanding performance on the examination. Roughly half of the students who do not achieve good results will enroll in technical and vocational schools"}]},"exams":{"text":"Shanghai's ninth-grade zhongkao is high-stakes, deciding entry to senior secondary school; the gaokao decides whether and where students attend college. Shanghai and Zhejiang piloted a revised gaokao from 2014.","cites":[{"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf","quote":"all students take the city-level graduation examination (zhong kao) at the end of ninth grade. This exam also serves as the selection test for senior secondary school, making it a high-stakes test as well."},{"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf","quote":"At the end of the senior secondary level (twelfth grade), students take a college entrance examination (gao kao) that determines whether and where a student will go to college"},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/shanghai-china/","quote":"In 2014, the MOE selected Shanghai and Zhejiang to pilot two versions of a revised gaokao."}]},"teachers":{"text":"Shanghai teachers develop in teaching-research groups and lesson observations; new teachers get a mentor for about three years, and upper-level teachers lead lesson development and support inexperienced colleagues.","cites":[{"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf","quote":"Important platforms for teacher professional development and performance evaluation\u2014teaching-research groups and lesson observations\u2014are also practiced universally in schools."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/shanghai-china/","quote":"In Shanghai, in addition to this training, all new teachers are assigned a mentor for about three years."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/shanghai-china/","quote":"Upper level teachers are responsible for leading the lesson development process, demonstrating effective lessons, and supporting unskilled or inexperienced teachers."}]},"tutoring":{"text":"Private tutoring, mostly for profit, was 'almost a household necessity' (OECD, 2011). After the 2021 'double reduction' policy, offline tutoring companies nationwide shrank 83.8%, the ministry reported in December 2021.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"private tutorials, most of them profit-making, are widespread and have become almost a household necessity"},{"url":"http://en.moe.gov.cn/news/press_releases/202307/t20230728_1071305.html","quote":"These policies, introduced in July 2021, are intended to ease the academic burden on K\u20139 students, including after-school tutoring."},{"url":"http://en.moe.gov.cn/news/press_releases/202201/t20220107_593036.html","quote":"visible progress was made in regulating out-of-school training, with academic subject-related tutoring significantly reduced. The number of offline training companies shrank by 83.8 per cent, and online ones by 84.1 per cent."}]}},"borrow":[{"text":"An OECD report (2011) highlights main lessons from China's reforms: an inclusive system in which all students are expected to perform at high levels, and greatly raising teacher pay and standards.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"The main lessons include the government's abandonment of a system built around \"key schools\" for a small elite and its development of a more inclusive system in which all students are expected to perform at high levels; greatly raising teacher pay and upgrading teacher standards and teacher education"}]},{"text":"A World Bank study (2016) credits Shanghai's teacher development system, with in-service training, school-based research groups, performance evaluation and a structured career ladder, as key to its excellence.","cites":[{"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf","quote":"Overall, Shanghai is characterized by a coherent and comprehensive system of teacher professional development that incorporates multiple layers of in-service training, school-based teacher research groups, evaluation of teacher performance, and a structured career ladder that provides both motivation and a mechanism for teachers to progress in their careers, which is key to Shanghai's demonstrated excellence in education."}]},{"text":"An OECD report (2011) describes Shanghai's 'commissioned administration,' in which good public schools take over weak ones, sending leaders and experienced teachers; a World Bank study (2016) calls this 'entrusted management' innovative.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"commissioned administration, a kind of school custody programme in which the government commissions \"good\" public schools to take over the administration of \"weak\" ones."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"the \"good\" public school appoints its experienced leader (such as the deputy principal) to be the principal of the \"weak\" school and sends a team of experienced teachers to lead in teaching"},{"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf","quote":"Innovative financing instruments such as \"entrusted management,\" which pairs low-performing schools with high-performing peer institutions, is rapidly gaining support."}]}],"tradeoffs":[{"text":"Shanghai students reported 14 hours of homework a week (OECD, 2014). Before China's 2021 'double reduction' policy, 46% of students said they could finish written homework in time for a good night's sleep, the ministry reported.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2014/12/does-homework-perpetuate-inequities-in-education_g17a258a/5jxrhqhtx2xt-en.pdf","quote":"students reported that they spend seven hours or more per week, on average, doing homework, while in Shanghai-China, students reported that they spend 14 hours per week, on average."},{"url":"http://en.moe.gov.cn/news/press_releases/202201/t20220107_593036.html","quote":"More than 90 percent of students reported they were able to finish written homework in time for a good night's sleep, as opposed to 46 percent before the introduction of the policy."},{"url":"http://en.moe.gov.cn/news/media_highlights/202107/t20210727_547230.html","quote":"Chinese authorities have introduced a set of guidelines to ease the burden of excessive homework and off-campus tutoring for students undergoing compulsory education."}]},{"text":"A World Bank study (2016) found the zhongkao tracks students with 'very little horizontal mobility' and suggested delaying tracking; in 2023 China's education minister said the 'intensive academic rat race' needs reversing.","cites":[{"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf","quote":"Consider delaying tracking to tenth grade or later. The zhong kao exam after ninth grade tracks students into three distinct senior secondary programs with very little horizontal mobility between programs."},{"url":"http://en.moe.gov.cn/news/press_releases/202307/t20230728_1071305.html","quote":"The minister stressed that political courage and long-term commitment are necessary to see through this historic reform to reduce educational inequality. The intensive academic rat race needs to be reversed to ensure balanced, equitable and quality compulsory education, he said."}]}],"sources":{"https://ncee.org/country/shanghai-china/":"Shanghai-China country profile (NCEE, archived 2025)","https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/643091467988910949/pdf/How-Shanghai-does-it-insights-and-lessons-from-the-highest-ranking-education-system-in-the-world.pdf":"How Shanghai Does It: Insights and Lessons from the Highest-Ranking Education System in the World (World Bank, 2016)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf":"Strong Performers and Successful Reformers in Education: Lessons from PISA for the United States, ch. 4 on Shanghai (OECD, 2011)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2014/12/does-homework-perpetuate-inequities-in-education_g17a258a/5jxrhqhtx2xt-en.pdf":"Does homework perpetuate inequities in education? PISA in Focus (OECD, 2014)","http://en.moe.gov.cn/news/media_highlights/202107/t20210727_547230.html":"China issues guidelines to ease burden of young students (Xinhua via Ministry of Education, July 2021)","http://en.moe.gov.cn/news/press_releases/202201/t20220107_593036.html":"MOE press conference on progress of the \"Double Reduction\" policy (Ministry of Education, December 2021)","http://en.moe.gov.cn/news/press_releases/202307/t20230728_1071305.html":"MOE convenes national conference to review progress on 'double reduction' (Ministry of Education, July 2023)"}}},"203":{"iso":"203","name":"Czechia","coverage":null,"summary":"Czechia lets top students split into an academic gymn\u00e1zium at age 11, far earlier than any U.S. school split.","different":["Unlike comprehensive U.S. middle/high schools, high-achieving Czech pupils can leave for an 8-year academic gymn\u00e1zium as early as age 11.","Most Czech upper-secondary students (about 72%) finish with the state Maturita exam; the U.S., by contrast, has no leaving exam and only optional SAT/ACT tests."],"common":["Like the U.S., Czechia splits school governance across national, regional and municipal authorities rather than running schools solely from the capital.","As in the U.S., Czech teachers typically need at least a bachelor's degree, often paired with separate pedagogical training, to be qualified."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"11","curriculum":"National framework programmes (Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports)","exit_exam":"Maturita"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the Education System and of its Structure - Czechia - Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/czechia/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Teachers in Europe: Careers, Development and Well-being - Eurydice Report","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2022-06/teachers_in_europe_2020_chapter_2_0_0.pdf"}]},"818":{"iso":"818","name":"Egypt","coverage":null,"summary":"Ministry of Education centrally sets curricula nationwide; students split into academic vs. technical/vocational secondary schools after grade 9.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Egyptian students are sorted after grade 9 (age 15) into general (academic) or technical/vocational secondary schools based on exam results.","Egypt runs a large parallel Al-Azhar religious school system from primary through secondary; the U.S. has no national religious school network.","Egypt's Ministry of Education centrally sets curricula, textbooks, and exams for public schools (Al-Azhar religious schools run separately), unlike the U.S., where states set standards."],"common":["Like the U.S., Egypt requires primary school teachers to hold a bachelor's degree in education before teaching.","Like the U.S., Egypt has a private school sector alongside public schools: 7.2% of primary pupils attended private school in 2005/06."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National - Ministry of Education determines curricula and textbooks nationwide","exit_exam":"General Secondary Education Certificate exam (Thanaweya Amma), end of 3-year general secondary"},"sources":[{"title":"Egypt; World Data on Education, 7th edition 2010/11 (UNESCO-IBE, revised May 2012)","url":"https://manshurat.org/file/4697/download?token=kwTZCGw6"}]},"268":{"iso":"268","name":"Georgia","coverage":null,"summary":"National curriculum set centrally by the Ministry; students may branch into vocational schools after grade 9 instead of general secondary.","different":["Georgia's Ministry of Education, Science and Youth sets one national curriculum for every school; the U.S. leaves curricula to individual states and districts.","After grade 9, Georgian students may choose a vocational institution instead of continuing to grades 10-12; the U.S. has no separate vocational school track."],"common":["Like the U.S. (about 9% private), roughly one in ten Georgian students attend private schools (210 of 2,296 schools in 2023).","As in the U.S., Georgia has no national school-leaving exam; its computer-based 12th-grade exit exams were abolished in 2019."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National - Ministry of Education, Science and Youth approves the national curriculum","exit_exam":"none (abolished 2019; previously a 12th-grade computer-adaptive exam)"},"sources":[{"title":"Georgia (TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia)","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Georgia.pdf"},{"title":"Law of Georgia on General Education","url":"https://matsne.gov.ge/en/document/view/29248?publication=68"}],"_audit":{"supported":9,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"348":{"iso":"348","name":"Hungary","coverage":null,"summary":"Kindergarten is compulsory from age 3 (primary at 6); students are tracked at 14 into academic vs. vocational secondary types.","different":["Hungary makes kindergarten compulsory from age 3; U.S. pre-kindergarten is optional, varies by state, and is not universally available.","At age 14, Hungarian students are sorted into academic grammar schools, vocational grammar schools, technicums, or vocational schools; the U.S. keeps one school type.","Hungary's \u00e9retts\u00e9gi exam both certifies secondary completion and serves as the university entrance exam nationwide; the U.S. has no equivalent national exam."],"common":["Like the U.S., Hungary requires primary school teachers to hold at least a bachelor's degree in education."],"facts":{"start_age":"3 (compulsory kindergarten); compulsory primary school starts at 6","tracking_age":"14","curriculum":"National - Ministry of Interior sets standards under the Act on National Public Education","exit_exam":"\u00e9retts\u00e9gi (upper-secondary leaving/matriculation exam)"},"sources":[{"title":"Hungary - Organisation and governance (Eurydice)","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/hungary/organisation-and-governance"},{"title":"Hungary - Overview (Eurydice)","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/hungary/overview"}],"_audit":{"supported":7,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"372":{"iso":"372","name":"Ireland","coverage":null,"summary":"A national curriculum and exam system; most schools are owned by religious or community patrons but funded by the state.","different":["Most Irish primary and secondary schools are privately owned by religious denominations or communities (patrons), though state-funded; U.S. public schools are owned by government districts.","Ireland funds two free pre-school years before compulsory schooling begins; U.S. public pre-kindergarten availability varies by state and isn't universal."],"common":["Like the U.S., Ireland's school types (secondary, vocational, community, comprehensive) share one post-primary structure: a three-year Junior Cycle from about 12, then Senior Cycle."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"none before 16","curriculum":"National - NCCA (National Council for Curriculum and Assessment) develops the curriculum","exit_exam":"Leaving Certificate"},"sources":[{"title":"Ireland - Organisation and governance (Eurydice)","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/ireland/organisation-and-governance"},{"title":"Ireland - Overview (Eurydice)","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/ireland/overview"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"398":{"iso":"398","name":"Kazakhstan","coverage":null,"summary":"Centralized Ministry of Education sets national curricula; after grade 9 students choose general, technical, or professional secondary tracks.","different":["Kazakhstan's Ministry of Education sets standard curricula and textbook requirements nationwide for every school, unlike the U.S.'s state-by-state standards.","After grade 9, Kazakhstani students choose general secondary education, technical education, or professional education; the U.S. keeps all students in one high school track.","Kazakhstani teachers undergo a national 'attestation' at least every 5 years, earning ranked categories from teacher to teacher-master tied to salary; U.S. licensing has no such ranking."],"common":["Like the U.S., Kazakhstan runs national standardized assessments in grades 4 and 9 to monitor school quality."],"facts":{"start_age":null,"tracking_age":"After grade 9 (basic secondary education)","curriculum":"National - Ministry of Education sets State compulsory education standards, curricula and textbook requirements","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Kazakhstan (TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia)","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Kazakhstan.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":9,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"428":{"iso":"428","name":"Latvia","coverage":null,"summary":"Latvia runs one national curriculum through age 16, then splits students into general or vocational upper-secondary tracks.","different":["Latvia's Ministry of Education sets one national curriculum and standards for all schools, unlike the U.S. where each state sets its own standards.","Latvia requires centralized state examinations (Latvian language, a foreign language, and mathematics are compulsory subjects) to finish upper-secondary school; the U.S. has no national leaving exam.","Latvia keeps all students in one integrated 9-year 'basic education' (ages 7-16) and only splits into general vs. vocational upper-secondary at 16."],"common":["Like the U.S., local governments (municipalities) operate public schools day-to-day, even though curriculum standards are set centrally rather than locally.","Like the U.S., a small share of schools (about 7% of Latvia's general schools) are privately run alongside the public system."],"facts":{"start_age":"5 (compulsory pre-school at 5\u20136; basic school from 7)","tracking_age":"none before 16","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education and Science sets standards and model programmes)","exit_exam":"Centralized state examinations (compulsory subjects include Latvian language, a foreign language, mathematics)"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the education system and of its structure - Latvia | Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/latvia/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Education system in Latvia | Izgl\u012bt\u012bbas un zin\u0101tnes ministrija","url":"https://www.izm.gov.lv/en/education-system-latvia"},{"title":"Assessment in general upper secondary education - Latvia | Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/latvia/assessment-general-upper-secondary-education"}],"_audit":{"supported":10,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"458":{"iso":"458","name":"Malaysia","coverage":null,"summary":"Malaysia's Ministry of Education centrally sets the curriculum and exam for national (government) schools, unlike U.S. state-by-state control.","different":["Malaysia's centralized Ministry of Education sets the national curriculum (KSSR for primary, KSSM for secondary) used in government schools; the U.S. has no national curriculum.","Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, Malaysian students in Forms 4-5 (about age 16) choose STEM or Arts/Humanities elective packages within their secondary school.","Malaysia's SPM exam at the end of Form 5 (about age 17) determines further study and job pathways; the U.S. has no equivalent national leaving exam."],"common":["Like the U.S. before any tracking, Malaysia keeps all students in the same lower-secondary school (Forms 1-3, to about age 15) before elective packages begin."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"16","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education sets KSSR/KSSM curricula; State Education Departments and District Education Offices implement locally)","exit_exam":"SPM (Sijil Pelajaran Malaysia), taken at end of Form 5"},"sources":[{"title":"Malaysia (TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia)","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Malaysia.pdf"},{"title":"Choosing academic streams - Malaysia.gov.my","url":"https://www.malaysia.gov.my/en/personas/parents-to-teenagers-13-17-years-old/menghantar-anak-ke-sekolah-menengah/choosing-academic-streams"}]},"496":{"iso":"496","name":"Mongolia","coverage":null,"summary":"Mongolia moved from a 10-year (age-8) system through an 11-year system (2005) to today's 12-year, age-6 system (2008).","different":["Unlike the stable U.S. K-12 system, Mongolia's school-starting age changed twice: 8 (10-year system), then 7 (2005), then 6 (12-year system, 2008).","Unlike most U.S. students, who attend a full K-12 school locally, students at many of Mongolia's rural 'soum' (district) schools must relocate, often boarding, to finish secondary."],"common":["Like the U.S., where public pre-K is common but not universal, 81% of Mongolian 15-year-olds recall attending pre-primary for a year or more."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National (State Education Standards set nationally; aimag Education Departments administer schools locally)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"PISA 2022 Results: Factsheets - Mongolia | OECD","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2023/11/pisa-2022-results-volume-i-and-ii-country-notes_2fca04b9/mongolia_a2a3f956/a93e1396-en.pdf"},{"title":"Mongolia; World Data on Education, 7th edition 2010/11 (UNESCO-IBE)","url":"https://daneshnamehicsa.ir/userfiles/file/Resources/18-1)%20Asia/Mongolia.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":6,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"807":{"iso":"807","name":"North Macedonia","coverage":null,"summary":"North Macedonia splits students after primary and lower-secondary school into 4-year academic gymnasiums or vocational tracks, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools.","different":["North Macedonia legally splits students after primary/lower-secondary school into 4-year academic 'gymnasium' schools or 2-4 year vocational schools; U.S. high schools do not sort students into separate school types.","About 70% of North Macedonia's upper-secondary students are enrolled in vocational programmes rather than academic gymnasiums, a much larger vocational share than in the comprehensive U.S. high school system.","North Macedonia legally provides primary schooling in five languages (Macedonian, Albanian, Turkish, Bosnian, Serbian); U.S. public schools instruct overwhelmingly in English, with some bilingual programs."],"common":[],"facts":{"start_age":null,"tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National","exit_exam":"State Matura (leads to higher education) or School/Vocational Matura (labour-market track), depending on programme"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation and governance - Republic of North Macedonia | Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/republic-north-macedonia/organisation-and-governance"},{"title":"Secondary education (or upper secondary education) and post-secondary non tertiary education - Republic of North Macedonia | Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/republic-north-macedonia/secondary-education-or-upper-secondary"},{"title":"Assessment - Republic of North Macedonia | Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/republic-north-macedonia/assessment"}],"_audit":{"supported":3,"corrected":3,"deleted":3}},"604":{"iso":"604","name":"Peru","coverage":null,"summary":"Peru's Ministry of Education sets one national curriculum, blending scientific, humanistic, and technical content for all secondary students, unlike the U.S.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Peru's Ministry of Education designs a national baseline curriculum that regions and individual schools then diversify for local needs.","Peru's Constitution and General Education Law declare Educacion Inicial (from age 3) compulsory, though the law phases in enforcement progressively; still younger than the U.S.'s 5-8 range."],"common":["Like the U.S., Peru does not divide secondary students into separate academic and vocational school types; its national curriculum blends scientific, humanistic, and technical content for everyone.","Like the U.S.'s local school districts, Peru delivers education through regional UGEL administrative units, even though curriculum is set nationally rather than locally."],"facts":{"start_age":"3 (Educacion Inicial; compulsory per Art. 12 and the Constitution, but the law phases in enforcement progressively)","tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education sets national curriculum; delivered via regional UGEL administrative units)","exit_exam":"none identified (university entrance exams are set individually by each university, not a unified national leaving exam)"},"sources":[{"title":"Ley N. 28044, Ley General de Educacion (Ministerio de Educacion del Peru)","url":"https://www.minedu.gob.pe/p/ley_general_de_educacion_28044.pdf"},{"title":"Peru. Reformas y cambios normativos en la educacion secundaria en los siglos XX y XXI (Consejo Nacional de Educacion / MINEDU)","url":"https://cdn.www.gob.pe/uploads/document/file/5888279/5217289-peru-reformas-y-cambios-normativos-en-la-educacion-secundaria-en-los-siglos-xx-y-xxi(2).pdf?v=1709069684"}]},"642":{"iso":"642","name":"Romania","coverage":null,"summary":"Unlike the decentralized U.S., Romania has a national curriculum, a three-track high school split, and a Baccalaureate exam for university entry.","different":["Unlike the U.S.'s locally chosen standards, Romania's Ministry of Education sets a national curriculum, implemented locally by county school inspectorates.","After grade 8 (about age 14-15), a national exam sorts Romanian students into academic, technological, or vocational high schools; the U.S. uses one comprehensive high school.","Most Romanian students take a national Baccalaureate exam, required for university entry; the U.S. has no national school-leaving exam."],"common":["Like the U.S., Romania's national system includes private and religious (confessional) schools operating alongside public schools."],"facts":{"start_age":"4 (compulsory pre-school, ages 4-6); primary begins around age 6","tracking_age":"14 (national exam after grade 8 sorts students into high-school tracks)","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education, implemented via county inspectorates)","exit_exam":"Baccalaureate Examination (Bacalaureat)"},"sources":[{"title":"Romania - Overview | Eurydice National Education Systems","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/romania/overview"}],"_audit":{"supported":6,"corrected":3,"deleted":0}},"702":{"iso":"702","name":"Singapore","coverage":null,"v2":{"rows":{"curriculum":{"text":"The Ministry of Education sets national curriculum policy; one framework, focused on problem-solving, guides math syllabi from primary to pre-university. Schools get significant autonomy in administrative and professional matters.","cites":[{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Singapore.pdf","quote":"MOE is responsible for selected functions within the education system to ensure that important and high-quality education resources are equitably distributed across schools. This includes setting and implementing national policies in critical aspects (e.g., curriculum, teacher recruitment and training, school admission criteria, funding rates, and fees payable)"},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Singapore.pdf","quote":"The Singapore Mathematics Curriculum Framework (see Exhibit 2) guides the development of mathematics syllabi at all grade levels, from primary to preuniversity education. It emphasizes the development of students\u2019 mathematical abilities, with a focus on problem-solving."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Singapore.pdf","quote":"MOE also devolves significant autonomy and responsibility to individual schools, within broad parameters, in school administration and professional matters"}]},"sorting":{"text":"PSLE results at the end of Primary 6 place students in secondary schools. From 2024, Full Subject-Based Banding began replacing Express/Normal courses; subjects like math come at three levels.","cites":[{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Singapore.pdf","quote":"At the end of Primary 6 (Grade 6), all students take the Primary School Leaving Examination (PSLE), the results of which are used for merit- and choice-based centralized placement into secondary schools."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Singapore.pdf","quote":"Prior to 2024, Secondary 1 (Grade 7) students enrolled in Express, Normal (Academic), or Normal (Technical) courses of study, designed to match student aptitudes and interests, and leading to the Singapore-Cambridge General Certificate of Education (GCE) Ordinary or Normal Level (O-Level or N-Level) qualifications. As of 2024, secondary schools have implemented Full Subject-Based Banding, starting with Secondary 1 students."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Singapore.pdf","quote":"Additionally, they take subjects such as mathematics, science, humanities, and languages at three different levels, allowing them the flexibility to pursue their strengths and interests in these subjects."}]},"exams":{"text":"National exams end primary (PSLE), secondary (O-/N-Levels, combined into one SEC certificate from 2027) and pre-university (A-Levels); results guide placement into the next stage's schools.","cites":[{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Singapore.pdf","quote":"National examinations aligned with the national curriculum are administered in the final years of primary, secondary, and preuniversity education to ascertain students\u2019 learning at the end of each key stage and to make decisions such as placement to schools in the next key stage."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Singapore.pdf","quote":"The Singapore Examinations and Assessment Board, in collaboration with MOE, conducts the following national examinations: the PSLE, GCE N-Level, GCE O-Level, and GCE A-Level."},{"url":"https://www.seab.gov.sg/secondary-education-certificate-sec/","quote":"In 2027, the Singapore-Cambridge General Certificate Education (GCE) for the N(T), N(A) and Ordinary Levels will be combined and renamed as the Singapore-Cambridge Secondary Education Certificate (SEC) in line with the implementation of Full Subject-Based Banding (FSBB)."}]},"teachers":{"text":"Teaching is highly regarded; candidates typically come from the top third of their cohort and face panel interviews. The Ministry sponsors training at NIE; subject groups plan lessons jointly.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/singapore/","quote":"The selection process is competitive: teaching is a highly regarded profession in Singapore, and teacher candidates are typically recruited from the top third of each academic cohort. The application process includes rigorous panel interviews that focus on the values, skills, and knowledge that make a good teacher"},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/singapore/","quote":"The National Institute of Education (NIE) is housed in Nanyang Technological University, one of the most prestigious institutions in Singapore\u2019s higher education system. Training is sponsored by the Ministry of Education."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/singapore/","quote":"All teachers are part of subject and grade-level professional groups in their schools. These groups collaboratively plan lessons, conduct classroom observations, share feedback, and work to improve learning across the subject or grade."}]},"tutoring":{"text":"Parliament heard in 2025 that a government survey found families spent S$1.8 billion on tuition in 2023; the ministry says some students take tuition 'even though they are coping well.'","cites":[{"url":"https://www.moe.gov.sg/news/parliamentary-replies/20250205-private-tuition","quote":"To ask the Minister for Education with reference to the latest Government survey on household expenditure which found that families in Singapore spent $1.8 billion on private tuition in 2023, an increase from previous years"},{"url":"https://www.moe.gov.sg/news/parliamentary-replies/20250205-private-tuition","quote":"MOE recognises that the reasons for taking up tuition are varied. While there are some students who genuinely need more dedicated help in coping with their studies, there are others who take tuition even though they are coping well."}]}},"borrow":[{"text":"An OECD report (2011) calls high-quality teachers and school leaders 'a major reason' for Singapore's high performance: recruits were selected from the top third of school graduates, and teachers were entitled to 100 hours of professional development a year.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"Since then, high-quality teachers and school leaders have formed the cornerstone of the education system and are a major reason for its high performance."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"Prospective teachers are carefully selected from the top one-third of the secondary school graduating class, by panels that include current principals."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"In recognising the need for teachers to keep up with the rapid changes occurring in the world and to be able to constantly improve their practice, they are entitled to 100 hours of professional development per year."}]},{"text":"An OECD report (2011) highlights that Singapore does not just set high standards and leave teachers to figure out how to meet them; it builds strong curricula and trains teachers to teach them.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"Singapore does not just establish high standards and then leave it to individual teachers to figure out how to achieve them. Serious attention to curriculum development has produced strong programmes in mathematics, science, technical education and languages, in particular, and has ensured that teachers are well-trained to teach them."}]},{"text":"An OECD report (2011) says larger, decentralized systems would need different mechanisms but must find ways to bring greater alignment; in Singapore, close attention to implementing each policy yields 'remarkable fidelity' and relatively little variation across schools.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"In Singapore, whenever a policy is developed or changed, there is enormous attention to the details of implementation \u2013 from the ministry of Education, to the National Institute of Education, cluster superintendents, principals and teachers. The result is a remarkable fidelity of implementation and relatively little variation across schools."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"While different mechanisms would be needed in larger, more multi-layered or decentralised systems, finding ways to bring greater alignment and to make all the parts work together is essential for producing results in the classroom in other nations\u2019 systems."}]}],"tradeoffs":[{"text":"NCEE reports Singapore is trying to curb its testing and achievement culture to lessen student stress and burnout, cutting tests across school levels, amid high depression, anxiety and stress among young Singaporeans.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/singapore/","quote":"Singapore has also focused on reducing the system-wide emphasis on testing and achievement culture in schools to lessen stress and burnout among students. The Ministry has reduced testing across school levels"},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/singapore/","quote":"These are part of broader initiatives the government is taking in response to high levels of depression, anxiety, and stress among young Singaporeans."}]},{"text":"Singapore's Ministry of Education notes government data show a gap in tuition spending across income groups, and warns excessive tuition 'can diminish students' joy of learning.'","cites":[{"url":"https://www.moe.gov.sg/news/parliamentary-replies/20250205-private-tuition","quote":"As Dr Wan Rizal and Mr Yip Hon Weng highlighted, the data from DOS also shows a disparity in tuition expenditure between households of different income quintiles."},{"url":"https://www.moe.gov.sg/news/parliamentary-replies/20250205-private-tuition","quote":"excessive tuition can diminish students' joy of learning and take time away from their holistic development. It can even be detrimental, when students struggle to cope with the additional demands of tuition."}]}],"sources":{"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Singapore.pdf":"Singapore chapter, TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia (IEA / Boston College, 2024)","https://www.seab.gov.sg/secondary-education-certificate-sec/":"Secondary Education Certificate (SEC) (Singapore Examinations and Assessment Board, 2026)","https://ncee.org/country/singapore/":"Singapore country profile (NCEE, 2025)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf":"Strong Performers and Successful Reformers in Education: Lessons from PISA for the United States (OECD, 2011)","https://www.moe.gov.sg/news/parliamentary-replies/20250205-private-tuition":"Private Tuition, parliamentary reply (Singapore Ministry of Education, 2025)"}}},"724":{"iso":"724","name":"Spain","coverage":null,"summary":"Like U.S. states, Spain's regions run schools under a national framework; tracking into academic/vocational paths starts later, at 16.","different":["Unlike the comprehensive U.S. high school, Spain splits students at age 16 (after compulsory ESO) into academic Bachillerato or vocational Formaci\u00f3n Profesional tracks.","Unlike the U.S.'s bachelor's-only requirement, Spain requires up to a master's degree to teach secondary school."],"common":["Like the U.S. (50 states), Spain shares control between a central government (basic framework) and autonomous communities (regions) that administer schools.","Like the U.S. (no school-type tracking before 16-18), Spain's 2020 reform removed formal academic/vocational tracks within compulsory secondary (ages 12-16)."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"16 (post-compulsory split into Bachillerato academic vs Formaci\u00f3n Profesional vocational)","curriculum":"Regional (autonomous communities), within a national framework","exit_exam":"Bachiller (Bachillerato diploma)"},"sources":[{"title":"Spain - Overview | Eurydice National Education Systems","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/spain/overview"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"764":{"iso":"764","name":"Thailand","coverage":null,"summary":"Unlike the U.S.'s decentralized system, Thailand's Ministry of Education centrally administers public school programmes nationwide.","different":["Unlike the U.S.'s 50 state systems with local districts, Thailand's Ministry of Education runs national programmes reaching public schools across the whole country."],"common":[],"facts":{"start_age":null,"tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education; via the Office of the Basic Education Commission)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Thailand's education system and skills imbalances: Assessment and policy recommendations (OECD Economics Department Working Paper No. 1641)","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2020/12/thailand-s-education-system-and-skills-imbalances-assessment-and-policy-recommendations_197225d8/b79addb6-en.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":0,"corrected":3,"deleted":6}},"858":{"iso":"858","name":"Uruguay","coverage":null,"summary":"Unlike the U.S.'s local districts, Uruguay's schools are run by one national body (ANEP), with no national exit exam for regular students.","different":["Unlike the U.S.'s locally run districts, Uruguay's public schools are run nationally by ANEP, an autonomous state education authority.","From the start of secondary school (about age 12), Uruguay splits students between academic liceos and separate technical UTU schools; the U.S. uses one comprehensive high school.","Uruguay's 14 years of compulsory education start at age 4, per ANEP, earlier than the U.S.'s 5-8 compulsory-starting range."],"common":[],"facts":{"start_age":"4","tracking_age":"12 (start of secondary: academic liceo vs technical/vocational UTU schools)","curriculum":"National (ANEP-designed curriculum)","exit_exam":"none for regular students (Bachillerato awarded through teacher/coursework assessment); a national accreditation exam exists only for adults 21+ outside the formal system"},"sources":[{"title":"Oferta educativa | ANEP","url":"https://pcentrales.anep.edu.uy/oferta-educativa"},{"title":"ANEP realizara Prueba Nacional de Acreditacion de Educacion Media Superior por primera vez | ANEP","url":"https://www.anep.edu.uy/anep-realizara-prueba-nacional-acreditacion-educacion-media-superior-por-primera-vez"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"032":{"iso":"032","name":"Argentina","coverage":null,"summary":"Argentina transferred national secondary schools to the provinces in 1992; compulsory schooling starts at 4, with pre-primary before primary.","different":["Argentina's national Ministry and Federal Council set common core curricular content nationwide (Ley 24.049); the U.S. has no national curriculum, just 50 state systems with local districts.","Since a 1992 law transferred school services to the provinces, national tax revenue still helps fund them, unlike U.S. local property-tax-funded districts."],"common":[],"facts":{"start_age":"4 (compulsory pre-primary/educaci\u00f3n inicial); primary begins at 6","tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"Mixed \u2014 national Ministry & Federal Council of Culture and Education set common core curricular content (Ley 24.049 Art. 22); provinces administer schools day-to-day","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Ley 24.049 (1992) \u2014 Transferencia de servicios educativos a las provincias, Argentina.gob.ar","url":"https://www.argentina.gob.ar/normativa/nacional/ley-24049-448/texto"},{"title":"Ley 27.045 (2014) \u2014 Obligatoriedad de la educaci\u00f3n inicial desde los 4 a\u00f1os, Argentina.gob.ar","url":"https://www.argentina.gob.ar/normativa/nacional/norma-240450/texto"}],"_audit":{"supported":0,"corrected":5,"deleted":4}},"048":{"iso":"048","name":"Bahrain","coverage":null,"summary":"Bahrain runs one free national school system through age 15; students then choose academic or vocational secondary tracks.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Bahrain has one Ministry-run national curriculum for all public schools, not locally chosen state standards.","Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, Bahraini students at 15 choose an academic (scientific/literary/commercial) or technical-vocational secondary track.","Unlike the U.S., Bahrain's Ministry of Education sets centralized final exams in grades 9-12, plus a national Grade 12 exam for university entry."],"common":["Like the U.S., Bahrain allows families to choose private, fee-based schools alongside free public schools."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education)","exit_exam":"General Secondary Certificate (based on MoE final exams through grade 12); separate BQA national exam for Grade 12 university entry"},"sources":[{"title":"Bahrain \u2014 TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Bahrain.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":7,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"100":{"iso":"100","name":"Bulgaria","coverage":null,"summary":"Bulgaria mostly funds schools through municipalities; a national exam around grade 7-8 sorts students into different secondary schools.","different":["Unlike the U.S., an external national assessment around grade 7-8 determines which academic (profiled) or vocational secondary school students attend.","Unlike typical U.S. teacher licensing, Bulgarian teachers need a Bachelor's, Master's, or Specialist degree in an approved teaching field."],"common":["Like the U.S., most schools are run and largely funded by municipalities (about 90% of kindergartens and primary schools), not the national government."],"facts":{"start_age":"7 (or 6 at parents' discretion)","tracking_age":"13-14 (national external assessment in grade 7 determines grade-8 placement)","curriculum":"National (state educational standards)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Bulgaria \u2014 Overview, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/bulgaria/overview"},{"title":"Bulgaria \u2014 Organisation of General Upper Secondary Education, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/bulgaria/organisation-general-upper-secondary-education"}],"_audit":{"supported":5,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"170":{"iso":"170","name":"Colombia","coverage":null,"summary":"Colombia's compulsory schooling runs nationally from age 5 to 16; basic education (9 grades) is followed by 2-year educaci\u00f3n media.","different":["Unlike the U.S., where compulsory schooling starts at 5\u20138 depending on the state, Colombia's is fixed nationally from ages 5 to 16.","Unlike the U.S., students finishing grade 11 take one national state exam, Saber 11, whose results give access to higher education."],"common":["Like the U.S., Colombian law requires parent and student representation on public-school governing boards."],"facts":{"start_age":"5","tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National (Ministry of National Education)","exit_exam":"Saber 11 (national state exam at the end of grade 11; results give access to higher education)"},"sources":[{"title":"Education at a Glance 2024: Colombia (OECD country note)","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2024/09/education-at-a-glance-2024-country-notes_532eb29d/colombia_cd55bf73/0eba06c5-en.pdf"},{"title":"Niveles de la educaci\u00f3n b\u00e1sica y media \u2014 Ministerio de Educaci\u00f3n Nacional de Colombia","url":"https://www.mineducacion.gov.co/portal/Preescolar-basica-y-media/Sistema-de-educacion-basica-y-media/233834:Niveles-de-la-educacion-basica-y-media"},{"title":"Ministerio de Educaci\u00f3n Nacional: El Icfes aplic\u00f3 el examen Saber 11 calendario A","url":"https://www.mineducacion.gov.co/portal/salaprensa/Comunicados/416365:El-Icfes-aplico-el-examen-Saber-11-calendario-A-la-prueba-de-Estado-con-mas-evaluados-del-ano"}]},"384":{"iso":"384","name":"C\u00f4te d'Ivoire","coverage":null,"summary":"C\u00f4te d'Ivoire runs one uniform French-language national curriculum; a national exam (BEPC) certifies lower-secondary completion.","different":["Unlike U.S. state-by-state standards, C\u00f4te d'Ivoire uses one uniform national math and science curriculum taught in French nationwide.","Unlike the U.S., which has no exam before grade 12, C\u00f4te d'Ivoire gives a national exam (BEPC) at the end of the first secondary cycle (Year 10).","Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, Ivorian students choose a science or literature track at the end of lower secondary (Year 10)."],"common":["Like the U.S., teacher preparation includes both initial training and ongoing in-service professional development."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15-16 (end of Year 10, first secondary cycle)","curriculum":"National (Ministry of National Education and Literacy - MENA)","exit_exam":"Brevet d'\u00c9tudes du Premier Cycle (BEPC, end of first secondary cycle, Year 10)"},"sources":[{"title":"C\u00f4te d'Ivoire \u2014 TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Cote-dIvoire.pdf"},{"title":"Direction des Examens et Concours (DECO), Minist\u00e8re de l'Education Nationale (MENAET-CI)","url":"https://www.men-deco.org/"}],"_audit":{"supported":7,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"222":{"iso":"222","name":"El Salvador","coverage":null,"summary":"El Salvador makes preschool (parvularia) compulsory from age 4; a national exam (PAES or AVANZO) is required for a bachillerato diploma.","different":["Compulsory education starts at age 4, two to four years earlier than in most U.S. states.","El Salvador requires students to pass a national exam (PAES or AVANZO) to graduate with a bachillerato diploma, per Art. 57.","Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, El Salvador's upper-secondary splits into a 2-year general track or a 3-year technical-vocational track."],"common":["Like federally mandated U.S. state tests, El Salvador's PAES/AVANZO exam is a required, standardized assessment for secondary graduates."],"facts":{"start_age":"4","tracking_age":"16 (start of educaci\u00f3n media: 2-yr general vs 3-yr t\u00e9cnico-vocacional track)","curriculum":"National (Ministerio de Educaci\u00f3n, Ciencia y Tecnolog\u00eda - MINED)","exit_exam":"PAES or AVANZO (national bachillerato exam, per Art. 57)"},"sources":[{"title":"Boleta de resultados PAES / AVANZO \u2014 Ministerio de Educaci\u00f3n, Ciencia y Tecnolog\u00eda","url":"https://www.mined.gob.sv/servicios/boleta-de-resultados-paes-avanzo/"},{"title":"El Salvador \u2014 Perfil de Pa\u00eds, SITEAL (UNESCO-IIEP)","url":"https://siteal.iiep.unesco.org/sites/default/files/sit_informe_pdfs/el_salvador_25_09_19_0.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":4,"corrected":5,"deleted":0}},"276":{"iso":"276","name":"Germany","coverage":null,"summary":"Germany sorts students into different secondary school types around age 10-12, depending on the state, after an 'orientation stage.'","different":["Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, German 10-12 year-olds are sorted into separate Hauptschule, Realschule, or Gymnasium school types.","Unlike locally chosen U.S. standards, each of Germany's 16 states (L\u00e4nder) sets its own curriculum, coordinated via a standing conference.","Germany's Abitur (required for university entry, earned after 12-13 years) comes mainly from Gymnasium, though other types also grant it, unlike universal U.S. diplomas."],"common":["Like the U.S., day-to-day school policy is set by regional (state) governments rather than the national government."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"10-12 (varies by state; primary runs grades 1-4 in most states, 1-6 in some)","curriculum":"Regional (16 L\u00e4nder, coordinated via KMK)","exit_exam":"Abitur (Allgemeine Hochschulreife)"},"sources":[{"title":"Germany \u2014 Secondary and post-secondary non-tertiary education, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/germany/secondary-and-post-secondary-non-tertiary-education"},{"title":"Germany \u2014 Organisation of the education system and of its structure, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/germany/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"}]},"352":{"iso":"352","name":"Iceland","coverage":null,"summary":"Iceland keeps all students age 6-16 in one unified school type, with no early academic/vocational split.","different":["Unlike Germany's early school-type split, Iceland keeps students in a single unified school through age 16, with no earlier separation."],"common":["Like the U.S., Iceland's system is predominantly public, with very few private schools."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"none before 16","curriculum":"National (National Curriculum Guide)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Iceland \u2014 Overview, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/iceland/overview"},{"title":"Iceland \u2014 Organisation of Single-Structure Education, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/iceland/organisation-single-structure-education"}],"_audit":{"supported":6,"corrected":0,"deleted":1}},"376":{"iso":"376","name":"Israel","coverage":null,"summary":"Israel's compulsory-education law spans ages 3\u201318 (enforced 5\u201316 today); the non-compulsory Bagrut exam certifies university-admission completion.","different":["Israel's compulsory-education law spans ages 3 to 18, but nationwide enforcement currently reaches only ages 5 to 16.","Unlike the U.S.'s single end-of-year tests, Israel's Bagrut matriculation exam is measured in cumulative subject 'units,' with just half of students passing it.","Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, about 40% of Israeli upper-secondary students take a vocational track instead of general academic study."],"common":["Like the U.S. testing math and language arts, Israel's Bagrut also assesses civics and literature \u2014 but sets exams nationally, not by state."],"facts":{"start_age":"5 (law specifies ages 3\u201318; only ages 5\u201316 currently enforced)","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education)","exit_exam":"Bagrut (matriculation exam, not compulsory)"},"sources":[{"title":"Israel \u2014 TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Israel.pdf"},{"title":"Education Policy Outlook: Israel (Country Profile) \u2014 OECD","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/about/projects/edu/education-policy-outlook/398023-Education-Policy-Outlook-Country-Profile-Israel.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"404":{"iso":"404","name":"Kenya","coverage":null,"summary":"Kenya is replacing its 8-4-4/KCSE system with a competency-based curriculum that sorts Grade 10 students into three pathways.","different":["Unlike stable U.S. state tests, Kenya is replacing its KCSE exit exam with a new national assessment for Senior School (CBC) cohorts.","Kenya's new curriculum groups Senior School students (from Grade 10, about age 15) into STEM, Social Sciences, or Arts and Sports Science pathways.","Curriculum is developed by one national body (Kenya Institute of Curriculum Development), not by separate states."],"common":["Like the U.S., Kenya has both free public basic education (about 20,000 public schools) and a sizable private school sector (about 8,500 private schools)."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15 (start of Senior School under the new 2-6-3-3 structure)","curriculum":"National (Kenya Institute of Curriculum Development)","exit_exam":"KCSE (for final 8-4-4 cohorts, being phased out); a new national assessment is being developed for CBC/Senior School cohorts"},"sources":[{"title":"[Original KICD and Ministry of Education Taskforce sources were unreachable/dead; replaced with:] Kenya: A Case Study \u2014 UNESCO Institute for Statistics (GAML)","url":"https://www.uis.unesco.org/sites/default/files/medias/fichiers/2025/09/GAML11_2.5.a_Kenya.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":7,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"422":{"iso":"422","name":"Lebanon","coverage":null,"summary":"Lebanon's private schools enroll about 60% of students, a falling share amid economic crisis, under one national MEHE curriculum.","different":["Unlike the U.S.'s roughly 9% private enrollment, about 60% of Lebanese students attend private school, though the share is falling as families shift to public schools amid the economic crisis.","One national ministry (MEHE) supervises curriculum for public and private schools alike, not separate state systems."],"common":["Like the U.S., all students share a common curriculum through grade 9 before any secondary-level specialization begins."],"facts":{"start_age":"3 (compulsory kindergarten, ages 3-5); basic education (Grade 1) begins around 6","tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education and Higher Education - MEHE)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Foundations for Building Forward Better: An Education Reform Path for Lebanon \u2014 World Bank (via UN Lebanon)","url":"https://lebanon.un.org/sites/default/files/2021-06/WB%20Report%20on%20EDUCATION_Foundations-for-Building-Forward-Better-An-Education-Reform-Path-for-Lebanon.pdf"},{"title":"Lebanon \u2013 TIMSS 2015 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timssandpirls.bc.edu/timss2015/encyclopedia/countries/lebanon/"}],"_audit":{"supported":5,"corrected":1,"deleted":3}},"470":{"iso":"470","name":"Malta","coverage":null,"summary":"Malta teaches in both Maltese and English; most students sit national SEC exams at the end of compulsory schooling.","different":["Unlike U.S. schools' single main language, Maltese students study in both Maltese and English throughout all 11 compulsory years.","Most Maltese students sit the national SEC exam at 16 as a prerequisite for further study, unlike the U.S.'s optional SAT/ACT.","After 16, Maltese students split into distinct general (Junior College) or vocational (MCAST) institutions, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools."],"common":["Like the U.S.'s local school districts, groups of Maltese schools (Colleges) have shared local management since 2005."],"facts":{"start_age":"5","tracking_age":"16","curriculum":"National (National Curriculum Framework, 2012)","exit_exam":"Secondary Education Certificate (SEC)"},"sources":[{"title":"Malta \u2014 Overview, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/malta/overview"}],"_audit":{"supported":7,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"499":{"iso":"499","name":"Montenegro","coverage":null,"summary":"Montenegro centrally runs one 9-year primary system; at 15 students choose gymnasium (academic) or vocational secondary school.","different":["Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, Montenegrin students choose a separate gymnasium (academic) or vocational school at about age 15.","Curriculum is set by one national Ministry of Education for the whole country, not by separate states."],"common":["Like the U.S., basic (primary) education runs 9 years before any high-school-level choice is made."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education, Science and Innovation)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Montenegro \u2014 Overview, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/montenegro/overview"}],"_audit":{"supported":7,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"578":{"iso":"578","name":"Norway","coverage":null,"summary":"Norway's counties, not the national government, run upper-secondary schools, where students choose from 15 general or vocational programmes.","different":["Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, 16-year-old Norwegians choose among 5 general or 10 vocational upper-secondary programmes.","Norwegian vocational programmes combine 2 years of school with 2 years of apprenticeship, unlike typical U.S. vocational classes.","Upper secondary is a guaranteed right rather than compulsory, and is run by counties, not the municipalities that run younger grades."],"common":["Like U.S. local districts, Norway's municipalities are responsible for primary and lower secondary schooling and counties for upper secondary, within a common national curriculum."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"16","curriculum":"National (common curriculum; run by municipalities/counties)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Norway \u2014 Overview, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/norway/overview"}],"_audit":{"supported":7,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"608":{"iso":"608","name":"Philippines","coverage":null,"summary":"A 2013 law added two years of senior high school (2016) to the Philippines, offering four tracks: academic, technical-vocational, sports, or arts.","different":["A 2013 law (RA 10533) added senior high school; the first grade-11 cohort started in 2016, extending high school by two years.","Unlike the many separate U.S. state and local bodies, one national agency (DepEd) sets curriculum for all Philippine public schools.","At grade 11, students choose among four tracks \u2014 Academic, Technical-Vocational-Livelihood, Sports, or Arts and Design \u2014 unlike typical U.S. electives."],"common":[],"facts":{"start_age":"5","tracking_age":"16","curriculum":"National (Department of Education - DepEd)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Republic Act No. 10533 (Enhanced Basic Education Act of 2013) \u2014 Official Gazette of the Republic of the Philippines","url":"https://www.officialgazette.gov.ph/2013/05/15/republic-act-no-10533/"},{"title":"Senior High School (SHS) Curricular Offerings \u2014 Department of Education (DepEd)","url":"https://sites.google.com/deped.gov.ph/wcsat/curriculum/shs-curricular-offerings"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":0,"deleted":1}},"643":{"iso":"643","name":"Russia","coverage":null,"summary":"Russia sets core curriculum standards federally, but regions and schools adapt materials; one exam (USE) ends school nationwide.","different":["Unlike U.S. state standards, Russia's schools follow Federal State Education Standards and model curricula; teachers pick textbooks from a Ministry-approved federal list.","Russian students completing grade 11 take the same federal Unified State Examination to finish upper secondary, unlike the U.S.'s optional SAT/ACT.","After 9 years (about age 15), students choose to continue general school or transfer to vocational institutions, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools."],"common":["Like the U.S., day-to-day school administration happens at the local level, within national standards set by federal authorities."],"facts":{"start_age":"6 (compulsory primary begins after non-compulsory preschool, ages 3-6)","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (federal standards, Ministry of Education)","exit_exam":"Unified State Examination (USE/EGE)"},"sources":[{"title":"Russian Federation \u2014 PIRLS 2021 Encyclopedia","url":"https://pirls2021.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Russian-Federation.pdf"}]},"703":{"iso":"703","name":"Slovakia","coverage":null,"summary":"Slovakia requires master's degrees for primary and secondary teachers (not kindergarten); students split into gymnasium, vocational, or arts tracks around 15.","different":["Unlike U.S. bachelor's-plus-license teachers, Slovak primary and secondary teachers must hold a master's degree plus pedagogical qualification.","Around age 15, Slovak students choose gymnasium (academic), vocational/industrial, or arts conservatory schools, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools.","One national ministry sets the compulsory content of the curriculum for the whole country, not separate states."],"common":["Like the U.S., municipalities run younger grades while a higher (regional) level of government runs upper-secondary schools."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (state educational programmes)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Slovakia \u2014 Overview, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/slovakia/overview"}]},"752":{"iso":"752","name":"Sweden","coverage":null,"summary":"Sweden's municipalities run and fund schools; at 16, students choose from 18 different national upper-secondary programmes.","different":["Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, 16-year-old Swedes choose from 12 vocational or 6 university-prep national programmes.","Sweden funds schools mainly through municipal taxes with national goal-setting, a more centralized policy layer than U.S. local property-tax funding.","Sweden trains teachers through four separate degree tracks (preschool, primary, subject, or vocational), more specialized than typical U.S. licensure."],"common":["Like the U.S., municipalities (roughly equivalent to school districts) run and fund day-to-day schooling, not the national government."],"facts":{"start_age":"6 (preschool class/f\u00f6rskoleklass, compulsory since 2018); compulsory school (grundskola) itself begins at 7","tracking_age":"16","curriculum":"National (central goals; run by municipalities)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Sweden \u2014 Overview, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/sweden/overview"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"792":{"iso":"792","name":"T\u00fcrkiye","coverage":null,"summary":"T\u00fcrkiye sorts students into different high school types using a national entrance exam after grade 8.","different":["Unlike the U.S., where course levels (honors, AP) vary within one school, Turkish students enter entirely separate high school types based on exam scores.","The Ministry of National Education centrally governs schools nationwide, unlike U.S. state- and district-level control."],"common":["Like the U.S., compulsory education totals 12 years, split into elementary, lower-secondary, and upper-secondary stages."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"14","curriculum":"National (Ministry of National Education)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"T\u00fcrkiye \u2014 TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Turkiye.pdf"},{"title":"T\u00fcrkiye \u2014 Overview, Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/turkiye/overview"}],"_audit":{"supported":6,"corrected":1,"deleted":2}},"860":{"iso":"860","name":"Uzbekistan","coverage":null,"summary":"Uzbekistan's general secondary runs one track to age 18 (specialized/vocational secondary exist too), ending in a final state certification exam.","different":["Unlike earlier tracking in some countries, Uzbekistan keeps students in one general school track through age 18 (11th grade).","Unlike frequent U.S. state testing, Uzbekistan's only final state certification exams come after grade 9 and again after grade 11.","One national ministry runs preschool and school education for the whole country, unlike U.S. state and local control."],"common":["Like the U.S., students can pursue academic university study or a technical/vocational diploma after finishing secondary school."],"facts":{"start_age":"7","tracking_age":"none before 18","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Preschool and School Education)","exit_exam":"Final state certification (end of grade 11)"},"sources":[{"title":"Uzbekistan \u2014 TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Uzbekistan.pdf"}]},"051":{"iso":"051","name":"Armenia","coverage":null,"summary":"Armenia runs one national school system with a single Ministry-set curriculum; the U.S. leaves standards and curriculum to states and districts.","different":["Armenia's Ministry of Education sets one national curriculum for all schools; the U.S. has no national curriculum, leaving standards to 50 state systems with local districts.","Students split at age 15 into senior secondary (academic) or vocational school after 9 years of basic school; U.S. high schools stay comprehensive, with no separate school types.","Armenia holds national school graduation examinations at Grade 12, run by its Assessment and Testing Center; the U.S. has no national exit exam, just a local diploma and optional SAT/ACT."],"common":["Like the U.S., Armenia provides 12 years of compulsory general schooling (primary, basic, and senior stages), similar to elementary, middle, and high school."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National \u2014 Ministry of Education, Science, Culture and Sports sets one curriculum for all schools","exit_exam":"Grade 12 school graduation examinations, administered by the national Assessment and Testing Center"},"sources":[{"title":"Armenia - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Armenia.pdf"},{"title":"Armenia inclusive education profile - UNESCO Global Education Monitoring Report 2020","url":"https://gem-report-2020.unesco.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/Armenia.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":9,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"056":{"iso":"056","name":"Belgium (Flemish Community)","coverage":"TIMSS, PIRLS, ICILS and PIAAC results cover Belgium's Flemish Community only; PISA 2025 covers all of Belgium. This note describes the Flemish system.","summary":"Flemish school boards set their own curricula and award diplomas directly; students split into four secondary-school tracks at age 14.","different":["Unlike U.S. states, which set common academic standards (often Common Core-based), Flemish school boards and private networks freely choose their own curricula and teaching methods.","Unlike the U.S.'s single comprehensive high school, Flemish students split at 14 into general, technical, arts, or vocational secondary schools.","Unlike U.S. diplomas tied to state tests, Flemish diplomas are awarded solely by each school's own class council (klassenraad) \u2014 there is no exit exam."],"common":["Like the U.S., there is no national curriculum \u2014 Flanders leaves curriculum decisions to individual school boards rather than an education ministry.","Like the U.S., Flemish families can choose among several publicly funded school networks: official, municipal/provincial, and private (mostly Catholic)."],"facts":{"start_age":"5","tracking_age":"14","curriculum":"Local \u2014 individual school boards/networks set curricula freely; government sets only conditions for school recognition and funding","exit_exam":"none (diploma awarded by the school's own class council/klassenraad, not a national exam)"},"sources":[{"title":"Overview","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/belgium-flemish-community/overview"},{"title":"Secondary and post-secondary non-tertiary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/belgium-flemish-community/secondary-and-post-secondary-non-tertiary-education"},{"title":"Assessment in vocational secondary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/belgium-flemish-community/assessment-vocational-secondary-education"}],"_audit":{"supported":9,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"116":{"iso":"116","name":"Cambodia","coverage":null,"summary":"Cambodia's Education Ministry sets national curriculum frameworks and a Grade 12 diploma exam, and is raising teacher standards to a bachelor's degree.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Cambodia's Ministry of Education, Youth and Sport adopts national curriculum frameworks and approves the syllabus for every subject and grade through upper secondary.","Unlike the U.S., Cambodia ends upper secondary with a national Grade 12 exam for the Upper Secondary Diploma; a Grade 9 exam, now run sub-nationally, ends lower secondary.","Unlike the U.S., where every teacher needs a bachelor's degree, Cambodia is still moving to that '12+4' standard: in 2022/23, 64.5% of public lower-secondary teachers held one."],"common":["Like the U.S., where schooling most often starts at 6, Cambodian law sets Grade 1 entry at age 6, though it guarantees nine free years rather than explicit compulsory schooling."],"facts":{"start_age":"6 (Grade 1 entry age under the 2007 Education Law; compulsory schooling not explicit in law)","tracking_age":"~15 (after Grade 9: upper secondary or TVET certificate courses)","curriculum":"National (MoEYS curriculum frameworks and syllabi for all grades)","exit_exam":"Grade 12 Upper Secondary Diploma Examination (baccalaureate)"},"sources":[{"title":"MoEYS \u2013 Education Strategic Plan 2024\u20132028","url":"https://moeys.gov.kh/storage/uploads/documents/67316f9d3a57f.pdf"},{"title":"UNESCO HerAtlas \u2013 Cambodia (Constitution Art. 68; Law on Education 2007, Arts. 31\u201332)","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/heratlas/country-overview?country=KH"},{"title":"World Bank \u2013 Cambodia education system and qualifications framework summary (2015)","url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/512181508744668918/pdf/120582-WP-P150980-PUBLIC-Cambodia-NQF-summary.pdf"}]},"188":{"iso":"188","name":"Costa Rica","coverage":null,"summary":"One national ministry sets curriculum for public education, and a national final-year test is required for the secondary diploma.","different":["Curriculum and standards are set nationally by the Ministerio de Educaci\u00f3n P\u00fablica, not by 50 separate states or ~13,000 local districts as in the US.","At around age 15, after lower secondary, students choose an acad\u00e9mica, t\u00e9cnica, or art\u00edstica branch of 'educaci\u00f3n diversificada'; the US keeps all students in one comprehensive high school track.","Unlike the U.S., Costa Rica has a separate three-year university degree, the Profesorado, reserved for training educators; it is shorter than the four-year bachillerato universitario."],"common":[],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"~15 (start of 'educaci\u00f3n diversificada': acad\u00e9mica/t\u00e9cnica/art\u00edstica branches, after 'educaci\u00f3n secundaria b\u00e1sica')","curriculum":"National \u2014 Ministerio de Educaci\u00f3n P\u00fablica (MEP), via the Consejo Superior de Educaci\u00f3n (CSE), sets policy and curriculum for official (public) education","exit_exam":"Pruebas Nacionales Estandarizadas Sumativas (subject tests ending Educaci\u00f3n Diversificada; 40% of final grade; results affect promotion and certification)"},"sources":[{"title":"Ministerio de Educaci\u00f3n P\u00fablica deja atr\u00e1s pruebas de Bachillerato","url":"https://www.mep.go.cr/noticias/ministerio-educacion-publica-deja-atras-pruebas-bachillerato"},{"title":"Gu\u00eda del Sistema Educativo de Costa Rica y Aspectos a Tener en Cuenta para el Reconocimiento de T\u00edtulos de Educaci\u00f3n Superior en Colombia","url":"https://contenidos.mineducacion.gov.co/ntg/men/micrositio_convalidaciones/Guias_mineducacion/MINEDU-costarica/assets/files/Guia_Sistema_Educativo_de_CostaRica.pdf"},{"title":"MEP: Estudiantes de duod\u00e9cimo a\u00f1o de colegios t\u00e9cnicos realizan Pruebas Nacionales Estandarizadas","url":"https://www.mep.go.cr/noticias/estudiantes-duodecimo-ano-colegios-tecnicos-realizan-pruebas-nacionales-estandarizadas"}]},"208":{"iso":"208","name":"Denmark","coverage":null,"summary":"Denmark's public Folkeskole schools most students together through age 16 (private alternatives exist), then splits them into general or vocational tracks.","different":["Unlike the U.S.'s track-free comprehensive high school, Denmark splits students at about 16 into separate general (gymnasium) or vocational upper-secondary school types.","Unlike the U.S.'s 50 different state standards, one national law (the Folkeskole Act) sets shared aims and standards for schooling across all Danish municipalities.","Unlike the U.S., which has no school-leaving exam, Danish upper-secondary students finish with a track-specific exam (STX, HHX, HTX, or HF)."],"common":["Like the U.S. (where districts run schools), Danish municipalities \u2014 not the national government \u2014 own and operate public schools day to day.","Like most U.S. states, compulsory schooling in Denmark begins around age six."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"16","curriculum":"National \u2014 the Folkeskole Act sets shared aims and standards; municipalities implement it locally","exit_exam":"No single national exam \u2014 upper secondary ends in track-specific exams (STX, HHX, HTX, or HF)"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation and governance","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/denmark/organisation-and-governance"},{"title":"Upper secondary and post-secondary non-tertiary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/denmark/upper-secondary-and-post-secondary-non-tertiary-education"}]},"233":{"iso":"233","name":"Estonia","coverage":null,"v2":{"rows":{"curriculum":{"text":"Uniform national curricula set content, learning objectives and time per subject for each grade span; schools split time by grade and write their own curricula from them.","cites":[{"url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/estonia/overview","quote":"Studies are conducted in ECEC institutions, general education schools and vocational schools \u2013 in case of the vocational upper secondary education \u2013 under uniform national curricula, on the basis of which schools compile their own curricula."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/estonia/","quote":"In addition to providing detailed content and learning objectives for traditional academic subjects, the new curriculum incorporated student competencies by grade span (grades one to three, four to six, seven to nine, and 10 to 12)"},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/estonia/","quote":"Estonia\u2019s curriculum specifies the amount of time to be spent on each subject at each grade span but leaves it to schools to organize grade-level allocations."}]},"sorting":{"text":"Grades one to nine form basic school; a few elite schools may use entrance exams. Upper secondary, academic or vocational, is compulsory from 2026 until 18 or a qualification.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/estonia/","quote":"Basic school, which includes both primary and lower secondary education in Estonia, covers grades one through nine. Upper secondary school, for which students choose either an academic or a vocational program, covers grades 10 through 12."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/estonia/","quote":"A very small set of elite public basic schools have additional admission criteria, which can include entrance exams."},{"url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/estonia/national-reforms-general-school-education","quote":"Students who complete basic education in spring 2026 or later are required to continue their studies in upper secondary general education or vocational education and training. Under the reform, compulsory participation in education has been extended until the age of 18 or until an upper secondary or vocational qualification has been obtained."}]},"exams":{"text":"Each upper secondary school sets admissions, which may use basic-school exam scores or entrance tests. Graduating requires state exams in mother tongue, math and a foreign language; universities consider results.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/estonia/","quote":"At the upper secondary level, each school sets its own admission criteria, which may include students\u2019 grades during basic education, scores on the basic school graduation exams or school-designed entrance exams, or interviews."},{"url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/estonia/assessment-general-upper-secondary-education","quote":"In order to graduate from upper secondary school, students have to pass state examinations in mother tongue or \u2013 in cases specified in the national curriculum for upper secondary schools \u2013 in Estonian as a second language, mathematics, foreign language, school examination and student study or practical work."},{"url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/estonia/assessment-general-upper-secondary-education","quote":"Upper secondary school examination results are taken into consideration for admission purposes at institutions of higher education."}]},"teachers":{"text":"Teachers need a master's degree with a research thesis; new teachers get mentoring. A 2026 career model ties salary to career stages; upper-grade subject programmes have more places than applicants.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/estonia/","quote":"revamped teacher education to incorporate learner-centered and digital practices, provide mentorships for new teachers, and require a master\u2019s degree with a research thesis"},{"url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/estonia/national-reforms-general-school-education","quote":"A career model for teachers has been introduced, establishing clear career stages and associated salary levels. The new model replaces the previous system, in which only a national minimum salary was defined, while actual teachers\u2019 salaries were determined by school heads."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/estonia/","quote":"There are more applicants than spaces in primary school teacher programs, but more spaces than applicants for subject-area teachers in upper grades."}]},"tutoring":{"text":"A 2026 Foresight Centre study found 28% of parents use private tutors: about a fifth of Estonian-speaking families but over half of Russian-speaking ones, amid the switch to Estonian-language schooling.","cites":[{"url":"https://arenguseire.ee/en/news/study-the-transition-to-estonian-language-based-education-has-led-russian-families-to-use-the-help-of-private-tutors/","quote":"In Estonia, 28% of parents use the help of private tutors to support their children, but differences between Estonian and Russian families are substantial"},{"url":"https://arenguseire.ee/en/news/study-the-transition-to-estonian-language-based-education-has-led-russian-families-to-use-the-help-of-private-tutors/","quote":"About one fifth of Estonian-speaking families use the help of private tutors, whereas more than half of Russian-speaking families have used such help. This indicates a growing need for support in learning the Estonian language resulting from the transition to instruction in Estonian."}]}},"borrow":[{"text":"OECD analysts say early childhood education can make systems more equitable; Estonia guarantees access to highly subsidized programmes, NCEE notes, and enrolled 91% of 3-year-olds in 2017 (OECD average: 79%).","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2020/12/education-policy-outlook-in-estonia_ddbaa07f/9d472195-en.pdf","quote":"Early childhood education and care (ECEC) policies can increase the equity of education systems."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2020/12/education-policy-outlook-in-estonia_ddbaa07f/9d472195-en.pdf","quote":"In 2017, Estonia\u2019s enrolment of 2-year-olds (73%) and 3-year-olds (91%) in ECEC was higher than the respective OECD averages of 62% and 79%."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/estonia/","quote":"guaranteed access to highly subsidized early childhood programs for all children; and strengthened vocational education and training."}]},{"text":"OECD analysts say managed school choice can favour equity; in Estonia, wider choice came with extra information for families and does not appear to have produced high segregation by international standards.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2020/12/education-policy-outlook-in-estonia_ddbaa07f/9d472195-en.pdf","quote":"According to OECD evidence, some system-level policies can favour equity, such as a longer period of compulsory education, delayed tracking, managed school choice and grade repetition."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2020/12/education-policy-outlook-in-estonia_ddbaa07f/9d472195-en.pdf","quote":"In Estonia, increased school choice has been accompanied by measures to provide additional information to parents and students to inform their choice of school and does not appear to have led to high levels of segregation in comparative terms."}]}],"tradeoffs":[{"text":"NCEE reports an aging teaching force and trouble recruiting; pay has risen but lags other professionals'. The EU's 2020 country report found half of teachers over 50.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/estonia/","quote":"Estonia\u2019s education system still faces challenges. As its teaching force ages, the country is struggling to recruit new teachers. Teacher salaries have risen in recent years, but they still lag behind those of other professionals in Estonia."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2020/12/education-policy-outlook-in-estonia_ddbaa07f/9d472195-en.pdf","quote":"The European Semester Country Report 2020 identified a number of key issues for Estonia in education and training:"},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2020/12/education-policy-outlook-in-estonia_ddbaa07f/9d472195-en.pdf","quote":"The emerging teacher shortages and the unattractiveness of the profession risk affecting the quality of education. Half of all teachers in primary and secondary education are over 50 years old and almost one in five teachers is over 60."}]},{"text":"In a 2026 Foresight Centre study, 41% of tutor-using parents cited insufficient support from school; its expert warns such tutoring aggravates inequality, as mainly wealthier families can pay.","cites":[{"url":"https://arenguseire.ee/en/news/study-the-transition-to-estonian-language-based-education-has-led-russian-families-to-use-the-help-of-private-tutors/","quote":"The study shows that 41% of parents who have used the help of a private tutor turn to them due to insufficient academic support from the school."},{"url":"https://arenguseire.ee/en/news/study-the-transition-to-estonian-language-based-education-has-led-russian-families-to-use-the-help-of-private-tutors/","quote":"If a school doesn\u2019t have a qualified subject teacher and parents need to hire private tutors to fill educational gaps, then the private tutoring market aggravates educational inequality,\u201d said Kindsiko. \u201cIt is primarily wealthier families who can afford the help of private tutors for their children."}]}],"sources":{"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/estonia/overview":"Estonia: Overview (Eurydice, 2026)","https://ncee.org/country/estonia/":"Estonia country profile (NCEE, 2025)","https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/estonia/national-reforms-general-school-education":"Estonia: National reforms in general school education (Eurydice, 2026)","https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/estonia/assessment-general-upper-secondary-education":"Estonia: Assessment in general upper secondary education (Eurydice, 2026)","https://arenguseire.ee/en/news/study-the-transition-to-estonian-language-based-education-has-led-russian-families-to-use-the-help-of-private-tutors/":"The Use of Private Tutors in Estonia, study release (Foresight Centre, 2026)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2020/12/education-policy-outlook-in-estonia_ddbaa07f/9d472195-en.pdf":"Education Policy Outlook in Estonia (OECD, 2020)"}}},"300":{"iso":"300","name":"Greece","coverage":null,"summary":"Greece runs one centralized system: the national ministry sets curricula and textbooks, and centrally-set Pan-Hellenic exams govern university entry.","different":["Unlike the U.S.'s 13,000 local districts, Greece's Ministry of Education centrally decides curriculum content, textbooks, and teaching time for every school.","Unlike the U.S.'s optional SAT/ACT, Greek university admission runs on centrally-set Pan-Hellenic Examinations taken at the end of upper secondary.","Unlike the U.S.'s comprehensive high schools, Greek students choose between a general Lykeio or a separate vocational Lykeio (EPAL) at about age 15."],"common":["Like U.S. high school diplomas, Greece's upper secondary certificate (Apolytirio Lykeiou) is issued by the student's own school, based on teachers' term marks and school exams."],"facts":{"start_age":"4 (compulsory pre-primary/nipiagogeio); primary at 6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National \u2014 Ministry of Education centrally sets curriculum content, textbooks, and teaching time","exit_exam":"Apolytirio Lykeiou (upper-secondary leaving certificate) plus nationally-set Pan-Hellenic Examinations for university entry"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation and governance","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/greece/organisation-and-governance"},{"title":"Organisation of the education system and of its structure","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/greece/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Assessment in general upper secondary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/greece/assessment-general-upper-secondary-education"}]},"360":{"iso":"360","name":"Indonesia","coverage":null,"summary":"Indonesia splits students into separate SMA (academic) or SMK (vocational) high schools after grade 9; the U.S. keeps one comprehensive high school.","different":["After grade 9, students choose separate SMA (general) or SMK (vocational) high schools; the U.S. has no school-type split, only in-school course-level tracks like honors or AP.","Indonesia has no mandatory national exit exam tied to graduation; the U.S. also has none but does use state tests."],"common":["Like the U.S., Indonesia has no mandatory national school-leaving exam tied to graduation; the new TKA assessment supports academic selection, not certification."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15-16 (after grade 9/SMP, split into SMA general vs SMK vocational)","curriculum":"National curriculum (Kurikulum Merdeka), overseen by the Ministry of Basic and Secondary Education (Kemendikdasmen)","exit_exam":"No national exam required for graduation; the TKA assessment supports academic selection/reporting, not graduation certification"},"sources":[{"title":"Ministry of Basic and Secondary Education (Kemendikdasmen) - official site","url":"https://kemendikdasmen.go.id/"},{"title":"Tes Kemampuan Akademik (TKA) 2025 - official TKA portal","url":"https://tka.kemendikdasmen.go.id/tka-2025/"}],"_audit":{"supported":3,"corrected":5,"deleted":1}},"380":{"iso":"380","name":"Italy","coverage":null,"summary":"Italy splits students at 14 into academic, technical, or vocational upper-secondary schools, unlike America's single comprehensive high school.","different":["Unlike the comprehensive U.S. high school, Italy sorts 14-year-olds into separate liceo (academic), technical, or vocational institutes after 3-year lower secondary school.","Curriculum guidance comes from the national Ministry, not U.S.-style state/local boards \u2014 though schools still have autonomy to define their own curricula and offerings.","Unlike the U.S., which has no national leaving exam, Italy's State-run general and vocational upper-secondary schools end with the Esame di Stato; regional IeFP tracks don't."],"common":["Like the U.S., Italy has both public (State) and private schools; recognized private 'paritarie' schools hold equal standing to State schools."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"14","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education and Merit sets guidelines; schools retain autonomy over curricula/offerings)","exit_exam":"Esame di Stato (state exam), also called esame di maturit\u00e0"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the education system and of its structure","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/italy/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Organisation of general upper secondary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/italy/organisation-general-upper-secondary-education"},{"title":"Organisation and governance","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/italy/organisation-and-governance"},{"title":"Assessment in general upper secondary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/italy/assessment-general-upper-secondary-education"}]},"XKX":{"iso":"XKX","name":"Kosovo","coverage":null,"summary":"Kosovo runs a national curriculum with a State Matura exam after grade 12; the U.S. has no national curriculum or leaving exam.","different":["Kosovo's Ministry of Education sets one national curriculum (the Kosovo Curriculum Framework) for all schools; U.S. states and local districts each set their own standards.","After Grade 9, Kosovo students choose gymnasium (academic) or vocational school around age 15; U.S. high schools remain comprehensive, with no separate school types.","Kosovo gymnasium students take a State Matura exam after grade 12 to graduate and enter university; the U.S. has no national school-leaving exam, only optional SAT/ACT."],"common":["Like the U.S., Kosovo's 12 grades split into primary, lower-secondary, and upper-secondary stages, similar to elementary, middle, and high school."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National \u2014 Ministry of Education, Science, Technology and Innovation sets the Kosovo Curriculum Framework","exit_exam":"State Matura examination (awards the State Matura Certificate), taken by gymnasium students after Grade 12; vocational students take a separate final exam"},"sources":[{"title":"Kosovo - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Kosovo.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":9,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"440":{"iso":"440","name":"Lithuania","coverage":null,"summary":"Lithuania assigns students to local schools by address like the U.S., but sets curriculum nationally and requires state Matura exit exams.","different":["Curriculum is set nationally by the Ministry of Education, unlike locally chosen U.S. state standards, textbooks, and courses.","Unlike the U.S., which has no national leaving exam, Lithuania's general (not vocational) upper-secondary students take state Matura exams across grades 11-12 for the Brandos atestatas.","A minority can instead choose vocational (VET) school partway through lower secondary rather than completing it at a general school."],"common":["Like most U.S. public-school assignment, Lithuanian municipal schools admit students by home-address catchment area rather than academic selection through lower secondary.","Unlike many European systems, Lithuania doesn't split students into separate academic/vocational schools by ability through lower secondary (age 16), similar to the comprehensive U.S. model."],"facts":{"start_age":"7 (pre-primary compulsory from 6)","tracking_age":"16 (VET optional partway through lower secondary; not ability-tracked before then)","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education sets curriculum framework/standards)","exit_exam":"Matura examinations (Brandos atestatas certificate)"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the education system and of its structure","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/lithuania/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Organisation of general lower secondary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/lithuania/organisation-general-lower-secondary-education"},{"title":"Secondary and post-secondary non-tertiary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/lithuania/secondary-and-post-secondary-non-tertiary-education"},{"title":"Assessment in general upper secondary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/lithuania/assessment-general-upper-secondary-education"}]},"480":{"iso":"480","name":"Mauritius","coverage":null,"summary":"A 2017 reform ended competitive exam sorting at 11; Mauritius assigns secondary schools by zone, delaying vocational tracking until after Grade 9.","different":["Since 2017, Mauritius assigns Grade 7 students to regional secondary schools using zone, parental choice, and PSAC score; the U.S. assigns by residential zoning alone.","Mauritius delays orientation toward technical/vocational schooling until after 9 years of basic education (about age 14-15); the U.S. rarely sorts students by school type at all.","At the end of Grade 9, Mauritius students sit the National Certificate of Education, which decides promotion and orientation into Grade 10; the U.S. has no comparable national exam."],"common":["As in the U.S., schooling is free and government-funded; Mauritius's Ministry of Education and Human Resources funds and runs state schools nationally."],"facts":{"start_age":"5","tracking_age":"14-15 (after Grade 9 basic education, via National Certificate of Education orientation)","curriculum":"National \u2014 Ministry of Education and Human Resources sets the National Curriculum Framework for all schools","exit_exam":"School Certificate (SC/O-Level) after Grade 11; Higher School Certificate (HSC/A-Level or HSC Professional) after Grade 13"},"sources":[{"title":"Nine-Year Continuous Basic Education (NYCBE) Booklet - Mauritius Ministry of Education","url":"https://education.govmu.org/Documents/educationsector/nys/Documents/NYCBE%20Booklet.pdf"},{"title":"9 Year Schooling Reform Brochure - Mauritius Ministry of Education","url":"https://education.govmu.org/Documents/educationsector/nys/Documents/9yearSchooling_Brochure.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"504":{"iso":"504","name":"Morocco","coverage":null,"summary":"Morocco requires national exit exams to advance at three points (grades 6, 9, 12); the US has none.","different":["Morocco requires a national exit exam at the end of primary (grade 6), lower secondary (grade 9), and upper secondary/baccalaureate (grade 12) \u2014 the US has none.","Curriculum is set nationally by the Ministry of National Education, then delivered through 12 Regional Academies that implement (not set) policy \u2014 unlike the US's locally chosen curricula.","Around age 15, after lower secondary, students choose upper-secondary tracks including science/humanities streams or technical-vocational options \u2014 the US keeps students in one comprehensive high school."],"common":["Compulsory schooling starts around age 6 with primary school, similar to most US states' typical kindergarten/first-grade start."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"~15 (end of lower secondary/grade 9; choice of general science/humanities vs technical-vocational upper-secondary streams)","curriculum":"National \u2014 Ministry of National Education, Preschool and Sports sets curriculum; 12 Regional Academies of Education and Training implement it locally","exit_exam":"Baccalaureate certificate (end of grade 12); separate national exit exams also end primary (grade 6) and lower secondary (grade 9)"},"sources":[{"title":"Morocco (TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia)","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Morocco.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":9,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"512":{"iso":"512","name":"Oman","coverage":null,"summary":"Oman's Education Ministry manages Grades 1\u201312; schooling is compulsory from age 6, and boys and girls attend separate schools from Grade 5.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Oman has a national Grade 12 exam, set and scored centrally and worth 70% of Grade 12 marks, for the General Education Diploma.","Unlike U.S. state attendance laws, Oman's 2023 national law makes basic education (Grades 1\u201310) compulsory from age 6; non-complying guardians can be fined or jailed.","Unlike mostly coeducational U.S. public schools, Oman's public schools are mixed only in Grades 1\u20134 (taught by women); from Grade 5, boys and girls attend separate schools."],"common":["Like U.S. public schools, Oman's government schools are free by law; private schools play a smaller role than in neighboring Gulf countries.","Like U.S. teachers, Omani teachers need at least a bachelor's degree, and the 2023 School Education Law bars anyone from teaching without a professional license."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"Grade 11 (~16): the post-basic stage (Grades 11\u201312) includes 'different educational tracks' under the 2023 law","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education develops curricula and schoolbooks; approves private schools' curricula)","exit_exam":"General Education Diploma (Grade 12 exams set and scored centrally; 70% of marks)"},"sources":[{"title":"Oman \u2013 TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Oman.pdf"},{"title":"Oman \u2013 PIRLS 2021 Encyclopedia","url":"https://pirls2021.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Oman.pdf"},{"title":"Royal Decree 31/2023 issuing the School Education Law (Ministry of Justice and Legal Affairs, Arabic)","url":"https://mjla.gov.om/laws/ar/1/show/195"}]},"616":{"iso":"616","name":"Poland","coverage":null,"summary":"Poland uses one 8-year primary school, then splits students into general, technical, or vocational secondary schools around age 15.","different":["Unlike U.S. comprehensive high schools, Poland sorts 15-year-olds into general, technical, or vocational secondary schools after one 8-year primary school.","Poland's 8th-grade exam results shape which secondary school type a student can enter, unlike U.S. grade-8 tests, which don't sort students into school types.","Final secondary exam is the national matura, required for university entry, unlike the U.S.'s optional SAT/ACT alongside grades."],"common":["Like the U.S., compulsory full-time schooling begins around age 6-7, combining one obligatory pre-school year with grade 1 at 7."],"facts":{"start_age":"6 (compulsory pre-school); primary at 7","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (core curriculum set by the Ministry of National Education)","exit_exam":"Matura (maturity exam)"},"sources":[{"title":"Key features of the Education System","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/poland/overview"},{"title":"Organisation of the education system and of its structure","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/poland/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":1}},"646":{"iso":"646","name":"Rwanda","coverage":null,"summary":"Rwanda teaches in English by law; national exams end primary, lower secondary and upper secondary, unlike the U.S.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Rwanda teaches in a language many teachers lack: its 2021 law makes English the medium, yet a 2018 study found 4% of primary teachers met English standards.","Unlike near-universal U.S. high school enrollment, Rwanda's secondary net enrollment rate is 29.1%, with over a million secondary-age children still in primary school.","Unlike U.S. comprehensive high schools, Rwandans who pass the national lower-secondary exam (about age 15) choose academic combinations, professional courses or TVET; another national exam ends upper secondary."],"common":["Like U.S. public schools, Rwanda's public and government-subsidised primary schools are free by law, and parents must enroll children once they reach school age (6).","Like U.S. pre-K, Rwanda's three-year pre-primary cycle (from age 3) sits outside compulsory schooling, which starts with primary; enrollment reached 44.7% in 2023/24."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"~15 (after the national lower-secondary exam: academic combinations, professional courses or TVET)","curriculum":"National (Competence-Based Curriculum, rolled out 2015; set by ministerial order; approved private schools may use international curricula)","exit_exam":"Advanced-level national examination at the end of upper secondary (Senior 6), with certificate"},"sources":[{"title":"Law N\u00b0 010/2021 determining the organisation of education (Official Gazette text, RwandaLII)","url":"https://rwandalii.org/akn/rw/act/law/2021/10/eng@2021-02-18/source.pdf"},{"title":"MINEDUC \u2013 Education Sector Strategic Plan 2024\u20132029","url":"https://www.mineduc.gov.rw/fileadmin/user_upload/Mineduc/Publications/PLANS/Education_Sector_Strategic_Plan/The_Education_Sector_Strategic_Plan__ESSP__2024-2029.pdf"}]},"705":{"iso":"705","name":"Slovenia","coverage":null,"summary":"Slovenia splits students at 15 into academic gimnazija or vocational/technical upper-secondary schools, each ending in a different matura exam.","different":["Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, Slovenia sorts 15-year-olds (after 9-year basic school) into academic gimnazija or vocational/technical school types.","Slovenia's basic-school curriculum is drafted nationally and formally adopted by the Minister of Education, unlike U.S. state/local curriculum control.","General-track students take the national 'matura' exam; vocational-track students take a separate 'vocational matura' \u2014 unlike the U.S.'s single, optional SAT/ACT path."],"common":["Like the U.S., municipalities are the formal local authority for basic schools (grades 1-9), similar to local school districts.","As in the U.S., preschool before compulsory school age is optional, not mandated nationwide."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (National Education Institute drafts, Council of Experts sets, Minister adopts basic-school curricula)","exit_exam":"Matura (general) or vocational matura"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the education system and of its structure","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/slovenia/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Organisation and governance","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/slovenia/organisation-and-governance"},{"title":"Teaching and learning in single-structure education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/slovenia/teaching-and-learning-single-structure-education"}],"_audit":{"supported":10,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"756":{"iso":"756","name":"Switzerland","coverage":null,"summary":"Switzerland has no federal ministry of education; the 26 cantons run and fund schools but must harmonize curricula by language region.","different":["Around age 12, Swiss cantons sort students by ability into different secondary-school tracks (streaming); U.S. students of all ability levels stay together in one comprehensive school.","At about age 15, when compulsory schooling ends, most Swiss teens enter apprenticeship-based vocational training (part school, part employer); the U.S. has no comparable widespread apprenticeship track.","Switzerland requires a cantonal Matura (or vocational Berufsmatura) exam at the end of upper secondary for university entry; the U.S. has no national school-leaving exam, just grades and often-optional SAT/ACT."],"common":["Like the U.S., Switzerland leaves most education decisions \u2014 funding, curricula, school structure \u2014 to sub-national governments (26 cantons) rather than a national ministry."],"facts":{"start_age":"4-5 (varies by canton)","tracking_age":"12","curriculum":"Regional \u2014 26 cantons set curricula; constitutionally required to coordinate and harmonize curricula and structure across language regions","exit_exam":"Matura/baccalaureate (academic track); Federal VET Certificate or Federal Vocational Baccalaureate (vocational track)"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the Education System - Eurydice Switzerland","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/switzerland/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Switzerland Overview - Eurydice","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/eurypedia/switzerland/overview"}]},"804":{"iso":"804","name":"Ukraine","coverage":"PISA 2025 scores cover 17 of Ukraine's 27 regions, as published by the OECD (\"Ukrainian regions (17 of 27)\"; PISA 2022 covered 18); the other regions were not tested during the war.","summary":"Ukraine requires a national test for university entry, unlike the optional U.S. SAT/ACT; PISA 2025 covered 17 of 27 regions.","different":["Ukraine's Ministry of Education and Science sets national education standards for all schools; U.S. states and local districts each set their own standards and curricula.","University-bound Ukrainian students must pass a mandatory standardized exam (NMT, formerly ZNO) for admission; U.S. college applicants submit grades plus an often-optional SAT/ACT score."],"common":["Like the U.S., Ukraine does not split students into separate school types before upper secondary (about age 15); younger students share general comprehensive schools."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15 (upper secondary: general academic vs vocational direction)","curriculum":"National \u2014 Ministry of Education and Science sets state standards for all schools","exit_exam":"National Multi-subject Test (NMT, formerly ZNO) \u2014 mandatory external exam for university admission"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the Education System - Eurydice Ukraine","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/ukraine/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"University Entrance Exams in 2024 - Ukrainian Center for Educational Quality Assessment","url":"https://testportal.gov.ua/en/university-entrance-exams-in-2024/"},{"title":"PISA 2022 results presentation - iea.gov.ua","url":"https://iea.gov.ua/en/7118-2/"},{"title":"OECD PISA 2025 Results, Annex B1 Table I.B1.2a (mean scores; \"Ukrainian regions (17 of 27)\")","url":"https://stat.link/mrq53f"}]},"704":{"iso":"704","name":"Vietnam","coverage":null,"summary":"Vietnam requires passing a national Grade 12 graduation exam (THPT) with a minimum score; the U.S. has no national school-leaving exam.","different":["Vietnam's Ministry of Education and Training sets one national curriculum (2018 General Education Program); the U.S. has no national curriculum, just 50 state systems."],"common":[],"facts":{"start_age":null,"tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National \u2014 Ministry of Education and Training sets the 2018 General Education Program as the national curriculum framework","exit_exam":"K\u1ef3 thi t\u1ed1t nghi\u1ec7p THPT (National High School Graduation Examination), a national exam administered by MOET"},"sources":[{"title":"Ch\u01b0\u01a1ng tr\u00ecnh gi\u00e1o d\u1ee5c ph\u1ed5 th\u00f4ng - MOET","url":"https://moet.gov.vn/tin-tuc-cot-phai/chuong-trinh-giao-duc-pho-thong"},{"title":"K\u1ef3 thi t\u1ed1t nghi\u1ec7p THPT n\u0103m 2025 - MOET","url":"https://moet.gov.vn/tin-tuc/ky-thi-tot-nghiep-thpt-nam-2025"}],"_audit":{"supported":3,"corrected":1,"deleted":4}},"036":{"iso":"036","name":"Australia","coverage":null,"summary":"States and territories run schools and set final-year exams; a national curriculum framework guides content from Foundation through Year 10.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Australia has a single national curriculum (the Australian Curriculum) for Foundation-Year 10 that all states adapt, rather than separate state standards.","About one-third of Australian students attend non-government schools, versus roughly 9% in the U.S.; these receive most public funding from the national government.","Unlike most U.S. states today, each Australian state runs its own formal Year 12 exams/assessments (e.g., Victoria's VCE) that certify graduation and set university entry."],"common":["Like the U.S., there is no national policy on tracking or ability streaming; grouping students by ability is a school-level decision, not a state or federal one.","As in the U.S., states/territories, not the national government, own and operate most schools and recruit teachers, though national bodies set shared professional standards."],"facts":{"start_age":"6 (5-6 depending on state)","tracking_age":"none (school-based decision only; no official state or national policy)","curriculum":"National + state (Australian Curriculum for Foundation-Year 10; states set Years 11-12 and certify exams)","exit_exam":"No single national exam; each state/territory examines and certifies Year 12 (e.g., VCE in Victoria)"},"sources":[{"title":"TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia: Australia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Australia.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"070":{"iso":"070","name":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","coverage":null,"summary":"No national ministry: 13 separate education ministries across entities, cantons, and Br\u010dko District each control curriculum.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Bosnia has no state-level ministry of education at all; 13 separate ministries in entities, cantons, and Br\u010dko District each run schools.","Unlike U.S. comprehensive high schools, Bosnian students split after 9th grade into general, vocational, arts, or religious secondary schools; vocational graduates can't go straight to university.","Unlike U.S. schools, some Bosnian schools segregate Croat and Bosniak students as 'two schools under one roof,' sharing a building but using separate entrances and curricula."],"common":["Like the U.S., curriculum and school governance are controlled locally/regionally rather than by national government, creating wide variation between areas."],"facts":{"start_age":"6 (compulsory pre-school from age 5 in FBiH/Br\u010dko District; not compulsory in Republika Srpska)","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"Regional/local (13 separate ministries; state-level agency APOSO sets a shared framework)","exit_exam":"None nationwide (some cantons, e.g. Tuzla, run their own matriculation exams)"},"sources":[{"title":"Bosnia and Herzegovina - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Bosnia-and-Herzegovina.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"124":{"iso":"124","name":"Canada","coverage":"TIMSS 2023 grade-4 results are based on 5 of Canada's 13 provinces/territories (Ontario, Quebec, Alberta, Manitoba, Newfoundland and Labrador); the rest were not sampled.","summary":"Canada has no federal education ministry; the 10 provinces and 3 territories independently run and fund their own separate school systems.","different":["Unlike the U.S. federal Department of Education, Canada has no national education ministry at all; provinces/territories hold exclusive constitutional authority over schools.","Unlike the U.S., several Canadian provinces (Alberta, Quebec, Newfoundland and Labrador, PEI) sort students into 'applied' or 'academic' tracks by Grade 10, called streaming."],"common":["Like the U.S., there's no national curriculum or leaving exam; some provinces (e.g., Alberta) require diploma exams while others don't, just as U.S. states vary.","As in the U.S., public schools are organized by local school boards, and instruction is offered in students' regional/official language(s), such as English or French immersion."],"facts":{"start_age":"6 (kindergarten mandatory only in New Brunswick)","tracking_age":"about 15-16 (Grade 10 in Alberta, Quebec, Newfoundland and Labrador, and PEI; Grade 11 in New Brunswick; Ontario ended Grade 9 streaming in 2022)","curriculum":"Provincial/territorial (no federal ministry); some shared regional frameworks (e.g., Western and Northern Canadian Protocol)","exit_exam":"None nationally; some provinces require diploma exams (e.g., Alberta's Grade 12 diploma exams count for 30% of the final grade)"},"sources":[{"title":"TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia: Canada","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Canada.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":2,"deleted":1}},"191":{"iso":"191","name":"Croatia","coverage":null,"summary":"National ministry sets Croatia's curriculum; after 8 years of single-structure school, students split into gymnasium, vocational, or art secondary schools around 14-15.","different":["At 14-15, Croatian students are split into academic gymnasiums, vocational schools, or art schools; the U.S. keeps students together in one comprehensive high school.","Croatia's Ministry sets a national curriculum for general schools (art schools use separate curricula); the U.S. has no national curriculum, and each state sets its own.","Croatian students who finish gymnasium or a 4-year program take the dr\u017eavna matura, a state exam required for university; the U.S. has no national leaving exam."],"common":["Like the U.S., local and regional authorities help finance schools and share responsibility for establishing them, alongside central government curriculum-setting."],"facts":{"start_age":"6-7","tracking_age":"14-15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Science, Education and Youth)","exit_exam":"Dr\u017eavna matura (state graduation exam)"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the education system and of its structure","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/croatia/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Single-structure primary and lower secondary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/croatia/single-structure-primary-and-lower-secondary-education"}]},"214":{"iso":"214","name":"Dominican Republic","coverage":null,"summary":"The Dominican Constitution makes schooling free and compulsory from preschool through secondary school, more than most U.S. states require.","different":["Unlike the single U.S. high school stage, Dominican secondary school splits into two 3-year cycles (ages 12-15, then 15-18) under a 2013 restructuring.","National Pruebas Nacionales exams count for 30% of the grade needed to finish basic and secondary school, unlike the U.S., which has no national school-leaving exam."],"common":["Like the U.S., where states require schooling into the teens, the Dominican Constitution also makes secondary school compulsory, not just primary."],"facts":{"start_age":"6 (primary); pre-primary also declared compulsory by the Constitution, but no specific starting age is given","tracking_age":null,"curriculum":null,"exit_exam":"Pruebas Nacionales (national tests at end of basic and secondary school, worth 30% of the final grade for promotion)"},"sources":[{"title":"Constituci\u00f3n de la Rep\u00fablica Dominicana (2015), Art. 63","url":"https://presidencia.gob.do/sites/default/files/statics/transparencia/base-legal/Constitucion-de-la-Republica-Dominicana-2015-actualizada.pdf"},{"title":"SITEAL/UNESCO-IIEP \u2013 Ordenanza 03/2013 (academic structure)","url":"https://siteal.iiep.unesco.org/bdnp/537/ordenanza-032013-modifica-estructura-academica-sistema-educativo-dominicano"},{"title":"MINERD \u2013 Manual de Aplicaci\u00f3n de las Pruebas Nacionales","url":"https://www.ministeriodeeducacion.gob.do/docs/manuales/N02k-manual-aplicacion-de-las-pruebas-nacionales-nivel-basico-y-nivel-mediopdf.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":1,"corrected":5,"deleted":3}},"246":{"iso":"246","name":"Finland","coverage":null,"summary":"Comprehensive local schools follow one flexible national curriculum; most teachers hold master's degrees; academic/vocational tracking begins later, at 16.","different":["Finland splits students at 16 into separate general (academic) or vocational upper-secondary schools; the U.S. keeps students in one comprehensive high school with course-level differentiation instead.","Finnish teachers in primary, lower-secondary, and general upper-secondary schools must hold a master's degree (vocational-track teachers may hold a bachelor's); U.S. teachers need only a bachelor's plus a state license.","Unlike the U.S., Finland sets national core curricula for basic and upper secondary schooling; in basic education, local authorities and schools then write their own curricula within that framework."],"common":["Like the U.S., Finnish schools are run mainly by local government (municipalities) rather than the national ministry, with schools and teachers given significant classroom autonomy."],"facts":{"start_age":"6 (compulsory education, incl. a pre-primary year); primary school itself begins at 7","tracking_age":"16","curriculum":"National core curriculum, high local/school autonomy","exit_exam":"Matriculation Examination (end of general upper-secondary)"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of single-structure education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/finland/organisation-single-structure-education"},{"title":"Teachers and education staff","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/finland/teachers-and-education-staff"},{"title":"Organisation of general upper secondary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/finland/organisation-general-upper-secondary-education"}]},"320":{"iso":"320","name":"Guatemala","coverage":null,"summary":"MINEDUC sets one national curriculum; the constitution requires bilingual teaching in areas with mostly-Indigenous populations.","different":["MINEDUC sets national curriculum for Guatemala's public schools, unlike the U.S., where states and districts set their own; private schools may use approved alternate programs.","Guatemala's constitution calls for bilingual teaching, preferentially, in areas with mostly-Indigenous populations; the U.S. sets no national curriculum requirements.","Secondary school splits into a B\u00e1sico (lower) cycle and a Diversificado (upper) cycle with separate diploma tracks, unlike the U.S.'s single comprehensive high school."],"common":["Guatemala's constitution requires the education system's administration to be decentralized and regionalized, similar to U.S. state education agencies."],"facts":{"start_age":null,"tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education - MINEDUC)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Constituci\u00f3n Pol\u00edtica de la Rep\u00fablica de Guatemala, Art. 76","url":"https://www.idpp.gob.gt/images/Biblioteca-virtual/Leyes_y_Reglamentos/Constitucin_Poltica_de_la_Repblica_de_Guatemala.pdf"},{"title":"Ley de Educaci\u00f3n Nacional, Decreto Legislativo 12-91, Art. 29","url":"https://goberchimaltenango.gob.gt/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/Ley-de-Educacion-Nacional.-Decreto-Legislativo-No.-12-91.pdf"}]},"364":{"iso":"364","name":"Iran","coverage":null,"summary":"Highly centralized: one Ministry of Education sets curriculum, textbooks, teacher hiring, and exams nationwide, unlike U.S. state/local control.","different":["Unlike U.S. local control, Iran's Ministry of Education centrally sets curriculum, textbooks, the school calendar, teacher hiring, and examinations for the whole country.","Unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools, Iranian students choose at age 15 among academic, technical-vocational, or knowledge-skill tracks; only the academic track leads toward university.","Unlike the U.S.'s lack of a national exit exam, Iran's upper-secondary exams count toward graduation and university admission, worth 40% of entrance scores in 2023, rising to 60% by 2027."],"common":["Like the U.S., preschool is optional and private schools enroll a minority of students; about 11.6% of Iranian students attend private school."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education / Organization for Educational Research and Planning)","exit_exam":"National final examination (end of grade 12)"},"sources":[{"title":"Islamic Republic of Iran - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Iran-Islamic-Rep.-of.pdf"},{"title":"Islamic Republic of Iran - PIRLS 2021 Encyclopedia","url":"https://pirls2021.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Islamic-Republic-of-Iran.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"392":{"iso":"392","name":"Japan","coverage":null,"v2":{"rows":{"curriculum":{"text":"MEXT sets the national curriculum (Courses of Study), revised about once a decade (latest rolled out 2020\u20132022), and approves textbooks; teachers may adapt material but are expected to follow it.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"The Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science, and Technology (MEXT) is responsible for the education system from early childhood to higher education, including setting the national curriculum for primary and secondary schools and approving aligned textbooks"},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"While teachers may adapt the material to suit classroom needs, they are expected to follow the national curriculum. MEXT revises the national curriculum about once every decade. Each new curriculum is rolled out in stages, with the most recent revision implemented from 2020 to 2022."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Japan.pdf","quote":"Under Japan\u2019s curricular reform, the national curricula, called Courses of Study, have been revised seven times since their implementation in 1947, with the goal of keeping up with societal changes over the years and the needs of each age group."}]},"sorting":{"text":"Public schools don't track students before upper secondary; math and science are taught in mixed-ability classes. Upper secondary starts in 10th grade, by entrance exam.","cites":[{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Japan.pdf","quote":"In public primary and lower secondary schools, there is no official policy on within-school streaming, and students are not tracked. From primary to the end of lower secondary school, a compulsory program of mathematics and science is taught to all students in mixed-ability classes."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Japan.pdf","quote":"Almost all prefectural boards of education administer the entrance examination for prefectural and municipal upper secondary schools, which students enter in 10th grade."}]},"exams":{"text":"Most upper secondary schools require entrance exams; the school a student attends is seen as a determining factor in later success. Universities use the national Common Test (since 2021).","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"The first major gateway in Japanese education is the entrance to upper secondary school. Most schools require entrance exams that are developed by the school or by localities."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"The school a student attends is considered a determining factor in later success."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"A revised national examination, called the Common Test, was introduced in 2021 and is designed to assess critical thinking, judgment, and expression with constructed response items, multiple choice questions, and an expanded English language writing and speaking skills section. While some universities and junior colleges require applicants to take additional entrance exams alongside the Common Test"}]},"teachers":{"text":"Candidates pass a prefectural hiring exam, often including a demonstration lesson, then a mentored one-year induction. Through lesson study, teachers plan lessons together and observe colleagues teach them.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"Once prospective teachers graduate from their program, candidates must pass a hiring exam overseen by the prefectural board of education. These assessments examine a candidate\u2019s pedagogical and subject area knowledge and often take the form of proficiency tests, essays, and interviews, as well as a demonstration lesson."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"After being hired, teachers undergo a one-year induction that includes on-site and off-site professional development, totaling about 300 hours, under the supervision of an experienced teacher who acts as a mentor."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"In addition to formal professional development programs, Japanese teachers use \u201clesson study\u201d to informally learn from colleagues. Principals organize meetings for teachers of varying levels of experience to identify classroom needs, research intervention options, and create a lesson plan that targets the need. One teacher then uses this sample lesson in the classroom, and the other teachers observe."}]},"tutoring":{"text":"Private tutoring schools (juku) are common, used to prepare for admission exams; a 2007 survey found attendance rising from 15.9% in grade 1 to 65.2% in the final junior-secondary year.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"More than half of Japanese students spend significant time each week in private tutoring centers, or juku, to prepare for key school and university admissions exams."},{"url":"https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/publication/29777/shadow-education.pdf","quote":"A 2007 survey found that juku served 15.9% of primary 1 children, that this proportion rose steadily in later grades, and that it reached 65.2% in junior secondary 3."}]}},"borrow":[{"text":"An OECD report (2011) highlights Japan's national curriculum: coherent, focused on core topics, logically sequenced, set at a very high level of cognitive challenge, and, it stresses, actually taught.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"The Japanese have paid more attention to the details of the national curriculum than most other countries and they have insisted that this curriculum is actually taught. The curriculum is coherent, carefully focused on core topics and their deep conceptual exploration, logically sequenced, and set at a very high level of cognitive challenge."}]},{"text":"An OECD report (2011) calls Japan 'a laboratory' for continuously improving teaching and says lesson study contributes in important ways to the high quality of instruction in Japanese schools.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"Japan is a laboratory for the idea of continuous improvement of teaching practice. The incarnation of that idea in Japanese schools is lesson study. This practice undoubtedly contributes in important ways to the high quality of instruction in Japanese schools."}]},{"text":"Japan rotates early-career teachers among schools every three to five years, letting prefectures put the strongest teachers in the highest-need schools, NCEE notes; the OECD (2018) finds disadvantaged students at least as likely as advantaged peers to get high-quality teachers.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"School assignments for teachers change every three to five years when they first start teaching, with fewer changes later in their careers. This practice allows the prefecture to assign the strongest teachers to the highest need schools and students."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2018/06/effective-teacher-policies_g1g8f550/9789264301603-en.pdf","quote":"This report shows that socio-economically disadvantaged students in Japan and Korea are at least as likely as advantaged students to be taught by high-quality teachers, as measured by characteristics such as years of experience, being certified for all the subjects taught"}]}],"tradeoffs":[{"text":"Juku are expensive, raising concerns about equal access and student well-being, NCEE notes; government efforts to limit attendance have not significantly reduced their influence, and MEXT has introduced volunteer-run community tutoring as an alternative.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"They have raised concerns about access for all students, as they are expensive. The Japanese government has tried a variety of policy responses to limit attendance at juku but has not succeeded in significantly decreasing their influence."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"This has raised significant concerns for the Ministry about equity of access and student well-being, given the societal focus on doing well on exams."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"MEXT has introduced public alternatives to juku, such as community-based after-school tutoring run by local volunteers and retired practitioners (Chiiki Mirai Juku), as part of a way to ensure access to support for those who want it"}]},{"text":"Full-time teachers reported 55.1 working hours a week in the OECD's TALIS 2024 survey (OECD average: 41), down 3.9 since 2018; NCEE lists less tolerance for long hours and heavy workloads among possible reasons for a teacher shortage.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/results-from-talis-2024-country-notes_e127f9e2-en/japan_b48b1dd7-en.html","quote":"Full-time teachers report that their total working hours per week is 55.1 hours (higher than the OECD average: 41). Teachers\u2019 total working hours have decreased by 3.9 hours since 2018."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/japan/","quote":"the country is now experiencing a teacher shortage despite falling student enrollment. Possible explanations include an aging teacher corps and a large wave of retirements, as well as less tolerance for the long hours and heavy workloads for educators, which have long been thought of as a moral duty of teaching."}]}],"sources":{"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Japan.pdf":"TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia: Japan (National Institute for Educational Policy Research, 2024)","https://ncee.org/country/japan/":"Japan country profile (NCEE, updated 2025)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf":"Strong Performers and Successful Reformers in Education: Lessons from PISA for the United States (OECD, 2011)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2018/06/effective-teacher-policies_g1g8f550/9789264301603-en.pdf":"Effective Teacher Policies: Insights from PISA (OECD, 2018)","https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/results-from-talis-2024-country-notes_e127f9e2-en/japan_b48b1dd7-en.html":"Results from TALIS 2024, Country notes: Japan (OECD, 2025)","https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/publication/29777/shadow-education.pdf":"Shadow Education: Private Supplementary Tutoring and Its Implications for Policy Makers in Asia (Bray and Lykins, Asian Development Bank, 2012)"}}},"414":{"iso":"414","name":"Kuwait","coverage":null,"summary":"Kuwait centrally sets one Arabic-medium national curriculum for public and Arabic-private schools; other private schools follow their home country's curriculum instead.","different":["Kuwait is highly centralized: a handful of Ministry of Education officials set policy nationwide, unlike the roughly 13,000 separate school districts that govern in the U.S.","Non-Arabic private schools teach math and science in English or their affiliate country's language and curriculum; Kuwait's public and Arabic-private schools use one Arabic national curriculum.","Kuwait has no teacher-education or professional-development requirement specific to math or science teachers, unlike the U.S., which requires a bachelor's degree plus a state license."],"common":["Like the U.S., where families can choose private, charter, or public schools, many Kuwaiti citizens opt for private school over free public education, aided by generous subsidies."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National (Arabic-medium, Ministry of Education); foreign private schools use their home country's curriculum except for Arabic","exit_exam":"Ministry-level secondary examinations (Grades 10-12, cumulative weighting, centrally scored)"},"sources":[{"title":"Kuwait \u2014 TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Kuwait.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":0,"deleted":1}},"442":{"iso":"442","name":"Luxembourg","coverage":null,"summary":"Core subjects are taught in German; French is added at 7 and Luxembourgish taught separately; students are tracked at 12.","different":["Luxembourg teaches most subjects in German, introduces French around age 7, and teaches Luxembourgish as a separate subject; the U.S. instructs almost entirely in English.","At age 12, Luxembourg sorts students into academic (classical, 7-year) or general secondary tracks; the U.S. keeps all students in one comprehensive high school.","Compulsory schooling starts at age 4 in Luxembourg; most U.S. states start compulsory schooling at age 6."],"common":["Like the U.S., local officials (communal mayors) handle school enrollment and compulsory-attendance compliance, alongside national funding and standards."],"facts":{"start_age":"4","tracking_age":"12","curriculum":"National (State-run public education)","exit_exam":"Diplome de fin d'etudes secondaires (secondary-leaving diploma, by track)"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the education system and of its structure","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/luxembourg/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Teaching and learning in primary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/luxembourg/teaching-and-learning-primary-education"},{"title":"Fundamental principles and national policies","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/luxembourg/fundamental-principles-and-national-policies"}],"_audit":{"supported":7,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"484":{"iso":"484","name":"Mexico","coverage":null,"summary":"Mexico's federal ministry (SEP) sets the national curriculum for basic education (preschool through lower secondary); states run day-to-day operations.","different":["Mexico's federal government sets the curriculum for basic education (preschool through lower secondary) nationwide, unlike the U.S. where each state sets its own standards.","Compulsory schooling legally starts at age 3 (preescolar) and runs through upper-secondary (bachillerato), unlike the U.S.'s 5-8 start and variable end age.","Around age 15, students choose among Mexico's three bachillerato streams (general, technical-vocational, or a mixed track), unlike the single comprehensive U.S. high school."],"common":["Like U.S. states, Mexico's 31 states have run basic schooling since 1992 decentralization, while the federal government sets curriculum and operates Mexico City's basic education.","Like the U.S., private schools operate alongside public ones and receive no public funding, attended by a minority of families."],"facts":{"start_age":"3","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (federal SEP)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"OECD \u2013 Education Policy Outlook: Mexico (2018)","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/about/projects/edu/education-policy-outlook/477789-Education-Policy-Outlook-Country-Profile-Mexico-2018.pdf"},{"title":"SEP \u2013 Art\u00edculo 3\u00b0 de la Constituci\u00f3n Pol\u00edtica de los Estados Unidos Mexicanos","url":"https://educacionbasica.sep.gob.mx/wp-content/uploads/2025/materiales/ARTICULO_3o.pdf"}]},"528":{"iso":"528","name":"Netherlands","coverage":null,"summary":"At about age 12, students are sorted by ability into separate secondary school types (VMBO/HAVO/VWO) leading to different further-education paths.","different":["At about age 12, Dutch students are sorted into distinct secondary tracks (VMBO, HAVO, or VWO) by ability, unlike the U.S.'s single comprehensive high school model.","Unlike the U.S., Dutch education includes both publicly and privately governed schools (often faith-based) operating within the same funded national system.","The Netherlands gives a national leaving exam (the central exam), identical for all students nationwide in the same track and subject; the U.S. has no equivalent."],"common":["Like the U.S., individual schools have autonomy over teaching methods and materials, even though the Netherlands sets core subjects and required hours nationally."],"facts":{"start_age":"5 (compulsory); most children start primary school at 4","tracking_age":"12","curriculum":"National (core subjects, hours, and school types set centrally); schools have autonomy on implementation","exit_exam":"Eindexamen (central exam): national, same-day exam per track (VMBO/HAVO/VWO); final grade averages it with the school exam"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the education system and of its structure - Netherlands","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/netherlands/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Administration and governance at central and/or regional level - Netherlands","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/netherlands/administration-and-governance-central-andor-regional-level"},{"title":"Assessment: General Secondary Education (HAVO, VWO) - Netherlands","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/netherlands/assessment-general-secondary-education-havo-vwo"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"275":{"iso":"275","name":"Palestine (Palestinian Authority)","coverage":null,"summary":"Palestine's Ministry of Education sets one national curriculum; students split into academic and vocational secondary streams by Grade 11.","different":["Lower-secondary students (Grades 8-9, around age 13) are already sorted into different school types by academic performance, unlike the comprehensive U.S. middle school.","Unlike the comprehensive U.S. high school, Palestinian students choose an academic (scientific/literary) stream from Grade 11, alongside vocational/applied streams offered from Grade 10.","Students take a national General Secondary Education Certificate Examination after Grade 12 for university admission; the U.S. has no equivalent national exit exam."],"common":["Like the U.S. (state tests in grades 3-8), Palestine runs its own periodic national assessments in math and science every two years since 2008.","Like the U.S., young students are promoted automatically in Grades 1-4 rather than held back, unless parents request grade repetition."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"13","curriculum":"National (Palestinian Curriculum Development Center, completed 2006)","exit_exam":"General Secondary Education Certificate Examination (after Grade 12)"},"sources":[{"title":"Palestinian National Authority - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Palestinian-National-Authority.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"620":{"iso":"620","name":"Portugal","coverage":null,"summary":"Portugal's Ministry sets curriculum nationally, except the 'own-curriculum' upper-secondary track; splits into academic, vocational, arts, or apprenticeship near 15.","different":["Portugal's Ministry of Education sets one national curriculum for every school; the U.S. has no national curriculum, and each of 50 states sets its own standards.","At about 15, after 9 years of compulsory basic school, Portuguese students choose science-humanities (academic) or vocational upper-secondary courses; the U.S. keeps all students in one comprehensive high school.","Portugal holds national final exams (exames nacionais) at the end of upper secondary, required for the academic track; the U.S. has no national school-leaving exam."],"common":["Like the U.S., compulsory schooling runs from about age 6 up to age 18 or completion of upper secondary, whichever comes first."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education, Science and Innovation)","exit_exam":"Exames Nacionais (national final exams, upper secondary)"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the education system and of its structure","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/portugal/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Organisation of general upper secondary education","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/portugal/organisation-general-upper-secondary-education"}]},"682":{"iso":"682","name":"Saudi Arabia","coverage":null,"summary":"Saudi Arabia sets one curriculum for public schools; compulsory schooling covers only ages 6-15, years short of most U.S. states' 16-18.","different":["Saudi Arabia's system is described as 'predominantly centralized': the Ministry of Education sets curriculum nationwide, unlike the U.S.'s roughly 13,000 locally governed school districts.","Compulsory education covers ages 6-15 only (through grade 9), several years short of the 16-18 end common in the U.S.; preprimary is optional.","Saudi Arabia introduced national teacher licensure only recently (2020s); some teachers still teach math or science provisionally without a subject degree, unlike U.S. certification norms."],"common":["As in the U.S., school type shapes curriculum: Saudi public, international, and religious schools each follow different content and assessment standards for math and science."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education sets standardized curriculum for public schools)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Saudi Arabia \u2014 TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Saudi-Arabia.pdf"}]},"710":{"iso":"710","name":"South Africa","coverage":null,"summary":"One national curriculum (CAPS) applies everywhere, but most children switch from home-language to English or Afrikaans instruction in 4th grade.","different":["Most children learn in their home language through 3rd grade, then switch to English or Afrikaans in 4th grade, unlike ongoing English-medium instruction in the U.S.","Public schools are ranked into five funding 'quintiles' by poverty level, so the poorest schools get more government money, the opposite of U.S. property-tax-based funding."],"common":["Like the U.S., South Africa runs alternative-certification routes: the TEACH SA program places non-education graduates in shortage subjects while they train part-time.","As in the U.S., public and independent (private) schools operate side by side, with independent schools required to meet comparable standards."],"facts":{"start_age":"7 (South African Schools Act, 1996); Grade R (age 5) was slated to become compulsory by 2022","tracking_age":"none before 16 (comprehensive; new 'Three Streams' academic/vocational pilot)","curriculum":"National (CAPS), delivered through 9 provincial departments","exit_exam":"National Senior Certificate ('matric')"},"sources":[{"title":"South Africa - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/South-Africa.pdf"},{"title":"South Africa - PIRLS 2021 Encyclopedia","url":"https://pirls2021.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/South-Africa.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":1}},"158":{"iso":"158","name":"Taiwan (Chinese Taipei)","coverage":null,"v2":{"rows":{"curriculum":{"text":"A national curriculum, the 12-Year Basic Education guidelines (2019), regulates all subjects in grades 1\u201312. In grade 8 science, teachers teach from textbooks, and all publishers cover the same topics.","cites":[{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Chinese-Taipei.pdf","quote":"The curricula of all subjects for Grades 1 to 12 (primary school to secondary school, including the first 3 years of vocational school) were regulated by the Curriculum Guidelines of 12-Year Basic Education, officially implemented in September 2019."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Chinese-Taipei.pdf","quote":"The national curriculum does not require topics of science knowledge for each grade in Stage IV. However, teachers teach based on textbooks, and all publishers of the Grade 8 textbooks select the same topics."}]},"sorting":{"text":"The nine compulsory years use district-based, single-type schools, where the law requires mixed-ability classes; at about 15, students enter general, vocational, comprehensive or specialized senior high schools.","cites":[{"url":"https://stats.moe.gov.tw/bookcase/Education_in_Taiwan/113/index.html","quote":"The first phase is the 9-year National Education, which is based on the Primary and Junior High School Act and Compulsory Education Act and applies to citizens aged six to 15. National Education is universal, obligatory, compulsory, free of charge, government-run in principle, school district-based, with open admission and single-type schools that offer general education."},{"url":"https://law.moj.gov.tw/ENG/LawClass/LawAll.aspx?pcode=H0070001","quote":"Schools shall form classes in all grades in such a way that each class reflects the norm-curve distribution. To at the same time accommodate students\u2019 adaptive development requirements, schools may implement group learning."},{"url":"https://stats.moe.gov.tw/bookcase/Education_in_Taiwan/113/index.html","quote":"Senior high schools in Taiwan include \u201cgeneral senior high schools,\u201d \u201cvocational senior high schools,\u201d \u201ccomprehensive senior high schools,\u201d and \u201cspecialized senior high schools.\u201d Students who graduate from junior high school or have an equivalent education level can gain admission to senior high schools through methods such as open admission and specialty enrollment."}]},"exams":{"text":"All grade 9 students sit a five-subject Comprehensive Assessment required for senior-high applications; university applicants via the Stars Program or Personal Application need a score on the five-subject GSAT.","cites":[{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Chinese-Taipei.pdf","quote":"Notably, at Grade 9, all students are mandated to take the Comprehensive"},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Chinese-Taipei.pdf","quote":"This assessment is a standard reference test covering Mandarin, English, mathematics, science, and social science. This assessment serves not only as an application requirement for senior high school"},{"url":"https://www.ceec.edu.tw/en/xmdoc/cont?xsmsid=0J180519762978782512","quote":"For students planning to participate in the Stars Program or the Personal Application process, a GSAT score is required. The GSAT includes five subjects: Chinese, English, Mathematics, Social Studies, and Science."}]},"teachers":{"text":"A teacher's certificate requires a bachelor's degree, pre-service teacher education, a qualification exam and a half-year full-time practicum; schools give new teachers one-on-one mentors.","cites":[{"url":"https://law.moj.gov.tw/ENG/LawClass/LawAll.aspx?pcode=H0050001","quote":"for each who was issued a Teacher\u2019s Certificate. 1.a bachelor's degree or higher; 2.a Pre-service Teacher Education Completion Certificate or any proof that they have completed Pre-service Teacher Education; 3.passed a Teacher Qualification Examination; and 4.completed the Practical Education Training with passing grades."},{"url":"https://law.moj.gov.tw/ENG/LawClass/LawAll.aspx?pcode=H0050001","quote":"a person who has passed a Teacher Qualification Examination may apply to a Teacher Education University to take a half year of full time Practical Education Training which includes teaching practice, class mentor practice, administrative practice, and research and study activities."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Chinese-Taipei.pdf","quote":"At the school level, there is a mentorship system to provide one-on-one guidance for new teachers, ensuring they have the necessary support to excel in their roles."}]},"tutoring":{"text":"Cram schools (buxiban) are widespread: in a 2001 survey, 72.9% of grade 7 students got tutoring, averaging 6.5 hours weekly; registered academic buxiban rose from 1,844 to 9,344 in 1999\u20132008.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/publication/29777/shadow-education.pdf","quote":"The education panel survey conducted in Taipei,China in 2001 covered 20,000 high school students and indicated that 72.9% of grade 7 students were receiving tutoring for an average of 6.5 hours per week (Liu 2012)."},{"url":"https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/publication/29777/shadow-education.pdf","quote":"in Taipei,China the number of universities and colleges expanded from 105 in 1999 to 162 in 2008, but the number of registered wen-li buxiban (literature and science cram schools) expanded from 1,844 to 9,344 (Kuan 2011:343)."}]}},"borrow":[{"text":"NCEE credits Taiwan's PISA 2012 reading gains partly to tighter teacher preparation: a 2005 ministry plan set out to evaluate preparation institutions, close weak ones and cut the number of teachers trained by 44%.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/2014/10/global-perspectives-explaining-taiwans-dramatic-improvement-in-pisa-reading/","quote":"In 2005, the Ministry introduced a plan to begin evaluating teacher preparation institutions, and close those judged to be inferior. The Ministry\u2019s goal was to reduce the number of teachers trained by 44 percent."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/2014/10/global-perspectives-explaining-taiwans-dramatic-improvement-in-pisa-reading/","quote":"monitored and adjusted the systems for preparing teachers so that only the most qualified candidates are licensed, and only the highest quality institutions confer degrees. Each of these improvements helps to explain the increases in Taiwan\u2019s reading scores on the 2012 PISA."}]},{"text":"NCEE reported in 2014 that Taiwan treats teacher evaluation largely as professional development, not a tool to weed out low performers: teachers are encouraged to observe one another, write feedback and co-develop improvement plans.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/2014/10/global-perspectives-explaining-taiwans-dramatic-improvement-in-pisa-reading/","quote":"In Taiwan, teacher evaluation is considered largely synonymous with teacher professional development, rather than a punitive tool to weed out low performers."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/2014/10/global-perspectives-explaining-taiwans-dramatic-improvement-in-pisa-reading/","quote":"Teachers are encouraged to observe one another, write up feedback and co-develop improvement plans, and meet in large groups to discuss how the results of the evaluation can be used as a tool for improvement."}]},{"text":"Taiwanese officials credit Happy Reading 101 (launched 2008), which funded more reading-instruction time, books and teacher training, with substantial reading gains, NCEE reports.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/2014/10/global-perspectives-explaining-taiwans-dramatic-improvement-in-pisa-reading/","quote":"These financial investments were announced as part of a literacy initiative called Happy Reading 101, launched in 2008. Taiwanese officials credit this program with Taiwan\u2019s substantial improvements in reading."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/2014/10/global-perspectives-explaining-taiwans-dramatic-improvement-in-pisa-reading/","quote":"Specifically, Happy Reading 101 provides funding to increase the amount of time dedicated to reading instruction in schools. It provides additional resources to improve reading instruction, including more and more updated books, lesson planning tools, and reading-focused educator professional development."}]}],"tradeoffs":[{"text":"Bray and Lykins (Asian Development Bank, 2012) report that many teachers lament cram-school pressure pulling children from wider personal and social development, and that reforms diversifying schooling did not decrease cram-school demand.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/publication/29777/shadow-education.pdf","quote":"Many teachers in Taipei,China and elsewhere also lament the fact that the pressures on children from cram schooling take them away from wider forms of personal and social development"},{"url":"https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/publication/29777/shadow-education.pdf","quote":"Government reforms in Taipei,China aimed to discourage narrow and passive learning habits by opening new pathways and diversifying the education system. However, rather than decreasing demand for cram schools, these reforms simply"}]},{"text":"Taiwan's TIMSS 2023 authors note that TIMSS 2011 exposed low student interest in math and science compared with other countries, wide rural-urban achievement gaps and less engaging eighth-grade teaching.","cites":[{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Chinese-Taipei.pdf","quote":"the subsequent TIMSS 2011 exposed persistent challenges. These challenges encompass low student interest in mathematics and science compared to other countries"},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Chinese-Taipei.pdf","quote":"wide rural-urban achievement gaps, less engaging teaching in eighth-grade mathematics and science, and a limited emphasis on scientific inquiry."}]}],"sources":{"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Chinese-Taipei.pdf":"Chinese Taipei chapter, TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia (IEA/Boston College, 2024)","https://stats.moe.gov.tw/bookcase/Education_in_Taiwan/113/index.html":"Education in Taiwan 2024/2025 (Ministry of Education, Taiwan, 2024)","https://law.moj.gov.tw/ENG/LawClass/LawAll.aspx?pcode=H0070001":"Primary and Junior High School Act, as amended 2023 (Laws & Regulations Database, Taiwan)","https://www.ceec.edu.tw/en/xmdoc/cont?xsmsid=0J180519762978782512":"General Scholastic Ability Test (GSAT): introduction (College Entrance Examination Center, Taiwan)","https://law.moj.gov.tw/ENG/LawClass/LawAll.aspx?pcode=H0050001":"Teacher Education Act, as amended 2019 (Laws & Regulations Database, Taiwan)","https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/publication/29777/shadow-education.pdf":"Shadow Education: Private Supplementary Tutoring in Asia, Bray & Lykins (Asian Development Bank, 2012)","https://ncee.org/2014/10/global-perspectives-explaining-taiwans-dramatic-improvement-in-pisa-reading/":"Explaining Taiwan's Dramatic Improvement in PISA Reading (NCEE, 2014)"}}},"784":{"iso":"784","name":"United Arab Emirates","coverage":null,"summary":"UAE public schools follow one federal curriculum; emirate bodies like Abu Dhabi's ADEK oversee private/international schools, which mostly set their own curricula.","different":["Public schools follow one federal Ministry curriculum, but Abu Dhabi (ADEK), Dubai (KHDA), and Sharjah also run authorities overseeing private schools, unlike uniform U.S. state systems.","From Cycle 3 (around age 14), public secondary students choose academic tracks (general, elite, advanced) or vocational tracks (e.g., aviation, engineering, agriculture), unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools.","Private schools (heavily used by expatriate residents) can choose their own curricula and are exempt from the national one, except in Arabic, social studies, and Islamic studies."],"common":["Like the U.S. (annual testing in grades 3-8 plus once in high school), the UAE uses a longitudinal standardized test (EmSAT) at multiple grades rather than one leaving exam."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"14","curriculum":"Federal for public schools (Ministry of Education); private schools largely choose their own, except Arabic/social studies/Islamic studies","exit_exam":"EmSAT Achieve (optional, Grade 12 / secondary completion)"},"sources":[{"title":"United Arab Emirates \u2014 TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/United-Arab-Emirates.pdf"},{"title":"Age limit for KGs and Grade 1 \u2014 U.AE","url":"https://u.ae/en/information-and-services/education/school-education-k-12/acceptance-kgs-and-grade-1"}],"_audit":{"supported":9,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"894":{"iso":"894","name":"Zambia","coverage":null,"summary":"Government schools follow a 7-2-3 grade structure with exams at each stage; schooling became tuition-free through Grade 12 in 2022.","different":["Zambia divides schooling into Primary (grades 1-7), Junior Secondary (8-9), and Senior Secondary (10-12), each ending in a national exam, unlike the U.S. K-12 structure.","By law, only primary school (grades 1-7) is compulsory in Zambia, unlike the U.S., where states require attendance well into the teens.","The Ministry of Education sets one national curriculum for all public schools, unlike the U.S., where each state and local district sets its own."],"common":["Like the U.S., basic education is publicly funded, and since 2022 it has been tuition-free all the way through Grade 12."],"facts":{"start_age":null,"tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education; national exams via Examinations Council of Zambia)","exit_exam":"Grade 12 School Certificate examination (Examinations Council of Zambia)"},"sources":[{"title":"Zambia Ministry of Education \u2013 National Education Policy (2025)","url":"https://www.edu.gov.zm/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/NATIONAL-EDUCATION-POLICY-2025.pdf"},{"title":"The Education Act, 2011 (Act No. 23 of 2011)","url":"https://www.parliament.gov.zm/sites/default/files/documents/acts/Education%20Act%202011.pdf"},{"title":"Examinations Council of Zambia","url":"https://www.exams-council.org.zm"}],"_audit":{"supported":9,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"040":{"iso":"040","name":"Austria","coverage":null,"summary":"Austria tracks students into academic or general secondary schools at age 10, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Austria sorts students at age 10 into academic (AHS) or general secondary schools (Mittelschule).","Unlike the U.S., Austria ends academic upper-secondary school and higher vocational colleges with a matriculation exam that qualifies students for university; the U.S. has no national exit exam.","Curricula are set nationally by the Federal Ministry, not by 50 separate states as in the U.S."],"common":["Like the U.S. with its states and districts, Austria's 9 provinces administer compulsory schools locally even though curriculum comes from the federal level."],"facts":{"start_age":"5 (compulsory pre-primary); primary at 6","tracking_age":"10","curriculum":"National (Federal Ministry sets curriculum; provinces administer schools)","exit_exam":"Matura (matriculation examination)"},"sources":[{"title":"Austria - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Austria.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":6,"corrected":3,"deleted":0}},"076":{"iso":"076","name":"Brazil","coverage":null,"summary":"Brazil splits schooling among federal, state, and municipal governments similarly to the U.S., but layers on a national curriculum floor (BNCC).","different":["Unlike the U.S., a National Common Curriculum Base (BNCC) sets required learning goals nationwide that states and cities must follow.","Brazil's national ENEM exam (optional) is used for university admission nationwide, unlike the U.S., where each state runs its own tests plus the optional SAT/ACT.","Basic education is compulsory from ages 4 to 17 in Brazil, starting earlier than most U.S. states' compulsory ages of 5 to 8."],"common":["Like the U.S., Brazilian states and municipalities hire teachers and set pay locally, causing large disparities in resources across the country."],"facts":{"start_age":"4","tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National floor (BNCC) implemented via states/municipalities","exit_exam":"None required; ENEM is optional, mainly for university admission"},"sources":[{"title":"Brazil - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Brazil.pdf"},{"title":"Exame Nacional do Ensino M\u00e9dio (Enem) - FAQ, INEP (Brazil Ministry of Education research agency)","url":"https://www.gov.br/inep/pt-br/acesso-a-informacao/perguntas-frequentes/exame-nacional-do-ensino-medio-enem"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"152":{"iso":"152","name":"Chile","coverage":null,"summary":"Chile centrally sets one national curriculum; most primary students attend state-subsidized private 'voucher' schools rather than fully public ones.","different":["Unlike the U.S., most Chilean primary students (about 53%) attend state-funded 'private subsidized' voucher schools rather than fully public or private ones.","Chile's Ministry of Education sets one national curriculum through Grade 10, then separate academic and vocational curricula for Grades 11-12, not 50 state standards as in the U.S.","Chile splits students into academic or vocational tracks in grades 11-12 (around age 16), unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools."],"common":["Like the U.S., Chile gives annual standardized tests (Simce) to monitor school and student performance nationally."],"facts":{"start_age":"5 (compulsory pre-primary/kindergarten); primary at 6","tracking_age":"16","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education)","exit_exam":"None required to graduate; PAES (Prueba de Acceso a la Educaci\u00f3n Superior), run by DEMRE, is a separate university admission test"},"sources":[{"title":"Chile - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Chile.pdf"},{"title":"DEMRE (Chilean university admissions department) - official site","url":"https://demre.cl/"}]},"196":{"iso":"196","name":"Cyprus","coverage":null,"summary":"Cyprus starts compulsory pre-primary education at age 4 years 8 months, years before the U.S. norm, though academic/vocational tracking waits until 15.","different":["Compulsory education starts with pre-primary at age 4 years 8 months in Cyprus (primary begins at 6), years earlier than most U.S. states' start of 5 to 8.","Cyprus centrally administers the Pancyprian Examinations, which serve as both the Lyceum/Technical School leaving exam and the public university entrance exam; the U.S. has no equivalent, using optional SAT/ACT instead.","At age 15, Cypriot students choose a general Lyceum or a technical/vocational school, a split most comprehensive U.S. high schools do not make."],"common":["Like the U.S., Cyprus provides free public education through upper secondary school, to about age 18."],"facts":{"start_age":"4 years 8 months (compulsory pre-primary); primary at 6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education, Sport and Youth)","exit_exam":"Pancyprian Examinations (centrally set): serve as both the Apolytirio leaving exam for Lyceum/Technical School and the public university entrance exam"},"sources":[{"title":"Cyprus - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Cyprus.pdf"},{"title":"Organisation of the Education System and its Structure - Eurydice Cyprus","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/cyprus/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Glossary - Eurydice Cyprus","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/cyprus/glossary"}],"_audit":{"supported":4,"corrected":5,"deleted":0}},"218":{"iso":"218","name":"Ecuador","coverage":null,"summary":"Ecuador runs one national school system with no state or local control, unlike the decentralized U.S. system.","different":["Unlike the 50 U.S. states, Ecuador's Ministry of Education sets one national curriculum and structure for the whole country.","Ecuador splits students into general (Ciencias) or technical Bachillerato tracks at age 15, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools.","Ecuador's 3-year Bachillerato (ages 15-17) is compulsory nationwide; the U.S. leaves both the length and start age (5 to 8) of compulsory schooling to each state."],"common":["Like the U.S., most Ecuadorian students attend comprehensive basic-education schools (bilingual and special-ed systems run parallel) before Bachillerato tracks begin at 15."],"facts":{"start_age":null,"tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education, Sport and Culture)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Bachillerato General - Ministerio de Educaci\u00f3n, Deporte y Cultura (Ecuador)","url":"http://web.archive.org/web/20260225030849/https://educacion.gob.ec/bachillerato-general/"}]},"250":{"iso":"250","name":"France","coverage":null,"summary":"France centrally sets curriculum and diplomas from Paris; the U.S. leaves these decisions to individual states and districts.","different":["Unlike the U.S., only France's national government may define school diplomas and curricula; local variation is minimal.","France requires a baccalaur\u00e9at exam (general, technological, or vocational) to finish upper-secondary school; the U.S. has no equivalent.","France starts compulsory schooling at age 3 (preschool), years earlier than most U.S. states' start of 5 to 6."],"common":["Like the U.S., most French students attend public school (86% primary, 79% secondary), with private schools mostly Catholic and state-monitored."],"facts":{"start_age":"3","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of National Education)","exit_exam":"Baccalaur\u00e9at"},"sources":[{"title":"France - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/France.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"344":{"iso":"344","name":"Hong Kong (China)","coverage":"Hong Kong is a Special Administrative Region with its own separate education system and government; results do not represent mainland China.","v2":{"rows":{"curriculum":{"text":"The Education Bureau sets the curriculum framework, eight learning areas with recommended time per subject; up to 25% of primary and 10% of lower-secondary time is left for school-arranged activities.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"The EDB sets the framework for the school curriculum in Hong Kong."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"The curriculum covers all subject areas for primary and secondary school. The framework is organized around eight Key Learning Areas (KLAs)"},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"The curriculum recommends learning time by subject, and up to 25 percent of time in primary school and 10 percent in lower secondary is set aside for schools to arrange flexible learning activities such as additional reading time, remedial or advancement activities, or cross-curricular learning."}]},"sorting":{"text":"Secondary schools first admit some pupils directly, often after interviews; the rest are split into three bands by academic performance and placed by parental preference and lottery, top band first.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"This system functions similarly to the system for primary schools except that schools, rather than the EDB, set criteria for admission in the first round, which often include an interview."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"Students who do not receive a place by direct application in the first round are divided into three groups based on academic performance and then assigned randomly within each group, with the top-performing group assigned first. Parent preferences are taken into account"}]},"exams":{"text":"Hong Kong's first territory-wide high-stakes exam, the HKDSE, ends upper secondary and is key to university entry; in just over half of subjects, school-based assessments count for 15\u201350%.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"There are no high-stakes, jurisdictional assessments in Hong Kong until the end of upper secondary school."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Hong-Kong-SAR.pdf","quote":"The senior secondary phase is particularly critical, as it culminates in the Hong Kong Diploma of Secondary Education (HKDSE), an essential qualification for university admission."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"In slightly more than half of all HKDSE core and elective subjects, there are also School-based Assessments (SBAs) that teachers must administer during the school year. SBAs count for between 15 percent and 50 percent of students\u2019 overall HKDSE results, depending on the subject."}]},"teachers":{"text":"Teaching is respected and well paid on a civil-service scale; registered teachers hold a degree plus teacher training, and teachers must complete 150 hours of professional learning every three years.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"Teaching is a respected profession in Hong Kong. Teachers are paid according to the Master Pay Scale of the Civil Service Bureau and are generally well-compensated compared to professionals with similar education."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"Candidates become either registered teachers, who have earned a teaching qualification by completing teacher training and hold a bachelor\u2019s degree or higher, or permitted teachers, who have a bachelor\u2019s degree but do not have formal teacher training."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"Hong Kong teachers are required to complete 150 hours of professional learning every three years."}]},"tutoring":{"text":"Tutoring is common: in 2011/12, 53.8% of surveyed grade 9 and 71.8% of grade 12 students were tutored, and NCEE says many still rely on it for competitive admissions.","cites":[{"url":"https://hub.hku.hk/bitstream/10722/184784/1/Content.pdf?accept=1","quote":"Among the Grade 9 students surveyed in the research reported in the present paper, 53.8% were receiving tutoring in 2011/12; and the corresponding figure for Grade 12 was 71.8%."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"Despite efforts to de-emphasize testing and provide more diverse pathways, a large percentage of students continue to rely on private afterschool tutoring to prepare for admission to secondary school and university which remain quite competitive."}]}},"borrow":[{"text":"An OECD report (2010) suggested using public exams 'in a positive way to facilitate learning', for example coupling them with school-based assessments and portfolios, as Hong Kong has experimented with.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/about/programmes/edu/pisa/publications/national-reports/pisa-2009/profiles-by-country-economy/Shanghai%20and%20Hong%20Kong%20PISA%202009.pdf","quote":"The PISA exercises and reformed public examinations in Shanghai, Hong Kong and Singapore all provide experimental ground for using public examinations in a positive way to facilitate learning."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/about/programmes/edu/pisa/publications/national-reports/pisa-2009/profiles-by-country-economy/Shanghai%20and%20Hong%20Kong%20PISA%202009.pdf","quote":"For example, public examinations could be coupled with school-based assessments, one-off examinations could be augmented by comprehensive and time-sensitive student portfolios and so forth. Many such dimensions are being experimented with in many systems, Shanghai and Hong Kong included."}]},{"text":"The OECD reports that Hong Kong abolished public assessments after primary school in 2001 and says the move proved critical, changing primary-school discourse from examinations and drills to learning.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/about/programmes/edu/pisa/publications/national-reports/pisa-2009/profiles-by-country-economy/Shanghai%20and%20Hong%20Kong%20PISA%202009.pdf","quote":"In 2001, as a first step in the reform, public assessments after primary schooling were abolished with immediate effect."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/about/programmes/edu/pisa/publications/national-reports/pisa-2009/profiles-by-country-economy/Shanghai%20and%20Hong%20Kong%20PISA%202009.pdf","quote":"However, the move has proved critical to primary schools, allowing teachers to develop more relevant school-based learning activities and changing the general discourse in primary schools from one of examinations and drills to one of learning."}]},{"text":"NCEE reports that Hong Kong regulates teacher-preparation places to match workforce demand, and says this means it is not, like many other jurisdictions, struggling with a teaching shortage.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/","quote":"Hong Kong regulates the number of available spaces in teacher preparation programs to ensure alignment with workforce demand. After two decades of expanding the teaching force, the number of places has been reduced in recent years given the declining student population. This means that Hong Kong is not, like many other jurisdictions, struggling with a teaching shortage."}]}],"tradeoffs":[{"text":"An OECD report (2010) said Hong Kong students 'suffer from tremendous examination pressure'; critics cited by Zhan and colleagues (2013) argue exam-oriented drilling in some tutoring undermines aspects of long-term learning.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/about/programmes/edu/pisa/publications/national-reports/pisa-2009/profiles-by-country-economy/Shanghai%20and%20Hong%20Kong%20PISA%202009.pdf","quote":"It is interesting to compare some of the common features of the two cities: they share a cultural heritage that treasures education, yet their students suffer from tremendous examination pressure."},{"url":"https://hub.hku.hk/bitstream/10722/184784/1/Content.pdf?accept=1","quote":"Critics argue that the examination-oriented drilling associated with some sorts of tutoring undermines dimensions of students\u2019 long-term learning"}]},{"text":"An OECD report (2010) argued that Hong Kong's emphasis on school autonomy leaves 'great disparity' among schools, with some publicly funded schools delivering sub-standard education.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/about/programmes/edu/pisa/publications/national-reports/pisa-2009/profiles-by-country-economy/Shanghai%20and%20Hong%20Kong%20PISA%202009.pdf","quote":"The philosophy of \u201cpositive non-intervention\u201d becomes a challenge because Hong Kong celebrates school autonomy and denounces anything that would downplay school-based endeavours. The result is great disparity among its schools."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/about/programmes/edu/pisa/publications/national-reports/pisa-2009/profiles-by-country-economy/Shanghai%20and%20Hong%20Kong%20PISA%202009.pdf","quote":"The result has been that some public schools receive standard public funding yet deliver sub-standard educational services. Parents see this as unfair."}]}],"sources":{"https://ncee.org/country/hong-kong/":"Hong Kong: education system profile (NCEE, accessed 2026)","https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Hong-Kong-SAR.pdf":"Hong Kong SAR chapter, TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia (IEA/Boston College, 2024)","https://hub.hku.hk/bitstream/10722/184784/1/Content.pdf?accept=1":"The Effectiveness of Private Tutoring: Students' Perceptions in Hong Kong, Zhan, Bray et al., Asia Pacific Education Review (2013)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/about/programmes/edu/pisa/publications/national-reports/pisa-2009/profiles-by-country-economy/Shanghai%20and%20Hong%20Kong%20PISA%202009.pdf":"Shanghai and Hong Kong: Two Distinct Examples of Education Reform in China, in Strong Performers and Successful Reformers in Education (OECD, 2010)"}}},"368":{"iso":"368","name":"Iraq (Kurdistan Region)","coverage":"Scores cover the Kurdistan Region only, an autonomous region in northern Iraq with its own education ministry, not federal Iraq.","summary":"The Kurdistan Region runs its own Kurdish-curriculum schools, separate from federal Iraq, teaching Kurdish, Arabic, and English together from basic school.","different":["Unlike federal Iraq or the U.S., the Kurdistan Regional Government's own Ministry of Education sets a distinct regional curriculum in Kurdish.","The Kurdistan Region compulsorily teaches Arabic and English alongside Kurdish from basic school onward, unlike the mostly English-only U.S. curriculum.","Basic education runs 9 years (Grades 1-9) before a separate 3-year preparatory stage splits toward higher education or vocational college, unlike the single U.S. comprehensive high school."],"common":["Like the U.S., teachers must hold a bachelor's degree, a requirement introduced in the Kurdistan Region's 2007 education reform."],"facts":{"start_age":null,"tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"Regional (Kurdistan Region's own Ministry of Education)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Kurdistan Region of Iraq - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Kurdistan-Region-of-Iraq.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":10,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"400":{"iso":"400","name":"Jordan","coverage":null,"summary":"Jordan centrally runs a 10-year compulsory 'basic education' cycle, then tracks students by their grades into different secondary school types.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Jordan tracks students by their grades into different types of schooling starting in grades 8 to 10 (about age 13).","Jordan's upper secondary (grades 11-12) is optional, not compulsory as in the U.S., and splits into comprehensive or applied/vocational streams.","Jordan requires the General Secondary Certificate Examination after 12 years of schooling for university admission; the U.S. has no equivalent."],"common":["Like the U.S. with its local school districts, Jordan decentralizes routine school administration even though curriculum is set centrally."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"13","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education)","exit_exam":"General Secondary Certificate Examination"},"sources":[{"title":"Jordan - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Jordan.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":7,"corrected":2,"deleted":0}},"417":{"iso":"417","name":"Kyrgyzstan","coverage":null,"summary":"Kyrgyzstan expanded compulsory schooling to 11 years in 2022; it still offers vocational secondary school as an alternative to general academic school.","different":["Kyrgyzstan's compulsory schooling now runs 11 years (grades 1-11); the U.S. leaves both the length and start age (5 to 8) of compulsory schooling to each state.","After grade 9 (around age 15), Kyrgyz students may continue into general secondary school or enter vocational/technical college instead; U.S. high schools remain comprehensive, with no separate school types.","Kyrgyzstan's Ministry of Education sets a national State Standard for general education; the U.S. has no national curriculum, just 50 state systems with local districts."],"common":["As in the U.S., vocational and technical programs exist alongside general academic secondary schools as an alternative path."],"facts":{"start_age":"6-7","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National \u2014 Ministry of Education sets a State Standard for general education","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Kyrgyzstan country overview - UNESCO HerAtlas","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/heratlas/country-overview?country=KG"},{"title":"Ministry of Education of the Kyrgyz Republic - official site","url":"https://edu.gov.kg/"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"446":{"iso":"446","name":"Macao (China)","coverage":"Macao is a Special Administrative Region with its own separate education system and government; results do not represent mainland China.","v2":{"rows":{"curriculum":{"text":"Schools have legal teaching autonomy and may use materials from outside Macao; the government set curriculum frameworks and attainment standards in 2014\u20132017; teachers chose materials for 90% of students (2022).","cites":[{"url":"https://pirls2021.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Macao-SAR.pdf","quote":"According to Article 122 of the Basic Law of Macao SAR, all educational institutions in the Macao SAR shall enjoy their autonomy and teaching and academic freedom and may continue to recruit staff and use teaching materials from outside the Macao SAR."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Macao-SAR.pdf","quote":"To steer the development of education in Macao, the DSEDJ introduced the Curriculum Framework for Formal Education of the Local Education System, Requirements of Basic Academic Attainments, and Curriculum Guides between 2014 and 2017."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2023/11/pisa-2022-results-volume-i-and-ii-country-notes_2fca04b9/macao-china_052fca91/5ab90f86-en.pdf","quote":"90% were enrolled in a school where teachers had the main responsibility for choosing which learning materials are used (OECD average: 76%)."}]},"sorting":{"text":"Vocational tracks start only at senior secondary. Many schools have required students who miss benchmarks to repeat grades; in PISA 2022, 22% of students had repeated one (OECD average: 9%).","cites":[{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Macao-SAR.pdf","quote":"Vocational and technical education is only available at the senior secondary level, whereas special education runs throughout infant, primary, and secondary education levels for students ages 3 to 21."},{"url":"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf","quote":"Due to lack of common assessment standards, each school had its own standard to assess the students, and many schools required students to repeat grades when they did not reach the required benchmarks."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2023/11/pisa-2022-results-volume-i-and-ii-country-notes_2fca04b9/macao-china_052fca91/5ab90f86-en.pdf","quote":"Some 22% of students in Macao (China) reported that they had repeated a grade at least once (OECD average: 9%) after entering primary school."}]},"exams":{"text":"A 2019 study points to an 'absence of high-stakes assessment'. Since 2017, four local higher-education institutions share a Joint Admission Examination; some students skip it through principal recommendation.","cites":[{"url":"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf","quote":"The first one is the low level of competition, as evidenced by mass higher education, numerous job opportunities, and absence of high-stakes assessment."},{"url":"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf","quote":"although some students are admitted to universities without taking the entrance examination by so-called \u201cprincipal recommendation scheme,\u201d other students still need to sit their examinations such as Joint Admission Examination for Macao\u2019s Four Higher Education Institutions (JAEM), which was first implemented in 2017."}]},"teachers":{"text":"Under a 2012 private-school framework, primary teachers need a higher diploma with teacher training; promotion through six ranks requires professional development, such as 90 hours for Level 6 to 5.","cites":[{"url":"https://pirls2021.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Macao-SAR.pdf","quote":"The framework was placed into effect on September 1, 2012, and was implemented in phases. According to the framework, to become a primary school teacher, one has to possess the following: A higher diploma in primary education or equivalent academic qualification or above that includes pedagogical training OR"},{"url":"https://pirls2021.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Macao-SAR.pdf","quote":"There are six levels of teaching staff rank in nontertiary education private schools in Macao, and promotion is based on service time, work performance, evaluation, and professional development."},{"url":"https://pirls2021.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Macao-SAR.pdf","quote":"to be promoted from Level 6 to Level 5, a teaching staff member is required to complete at least 90 hours of professional development activities during the three years of actual service at Level 6."}]},"tutoring":{"text":"A 2008 study reported by Macao's education bureau found 20% of students took private tutoring outside school and around 40% attended homework-supervision classes inside schools, which may charge fees.","cites":[{"url":"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf","quote":"In 2008, a large-scale study conducted by the Chinese University of Hong Kong and reported by the Macau Education and Youth Affairs Bureau (Ho & Kwong, 2008) was the first in-depth study on shadow education in Macao."},{"url":"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf","quote":"Of all primary and secondary students, around 40% were reported to participate in homework supervision classes within schools, and 20% received private supplementary tutoring in centers or by individuals outside schools (p. ii)."},{"url":"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf","quote":"Besides private tutoring centers, schools in Macao can legitimately run fee-paying after-school tutoring classes."}]}},"borrow":[{"text":"Researcher Vivien Chan (2019) suggests the free or low-cost after-school remedial classes some Macao schools run with government Education Development Fund money, taught by paid teachers, could be seen as 'a proactive approach to combating educational inequalities.'","cites":[{"url":"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf","quote":"Some Macao schools offer fee-free after-school remedial classes, using the budget from the DSEJ\u2019s Education Development Fund. The purpose of this scheme is to help students who have difficulty in studies to receive immediate academic assistance at nominal charges or sometimes free of charge"},{"url":"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf","quote":"Teachers who teach these classes are paid. This scheme could be seen as a proactive approach to combating educational inequalities."}]},{"text":"Macao's education bureau (DSEJ) credits its PISA 2018 progress partly to policies including remedial and enrichment programs and fostering reading skills, and notes many teachers form professional learning communities.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.gov.mo/en/news/120558/","quote":"The progress as revealed in the PISA assessment reflects that the policies promoted by educational practitioners and the Macao government bear fruits and attain effectiveness."},{"url":"https://www.gov.mo/en/news/120558/","quote":"Such policies, amongst others, include higher level of education provision and instructional support, establishment of remedial and enrichment programs, setting up platforms nourishing inner potentials, promotion of student well-being and creative thinking, fostering of reading and learning skills, etc."},{"url":"https://www.gov.mo/en/news/120558/","quote":"Many Macao teachers form professional learning communities to enhance exchanges and learning opportunities."}]}],"tradeoffs":[{"text":"Researchers Ho and Kwong (2008) concluded exam pressure was one of the main reasons tutoring intensified in Macao; some tutoring vendors use slogans such as 'life-or-death test' to attract students.","cites":[{"url":"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf","quote":"Ho and Kwong (2008) concluded that examination pressure was one of the main reasons for intensifying tutoring (p. 65)."},{"url":"https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf","quote":"Some tutoring vendors even use appalling slogans such as \u201clife-or-death test\u201d and \u201conce failed, all lost\u201d to attract more students to sign up for classes (Macao Daily News, 2016, p. A1)."}]}],"sources":{"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Macao-SAR.pdf":"Macao SAR chapter, TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia (IEA/Boston College, 2024)","https://pirls2021.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Macao-SAR.pdf":"Macao SAR chapter, PIRLS 2021 Encyclopedia (IEA/Boston College, 2022)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2023/11/pisa-2022-results-volume-i-and-ii-country-notes_2fca04b9/macao-china_052fca91/5ab90f86-en.pdf":"PISA 2022 Results (Volumes I and II) country note: Macao (China) (OECD, 2023)","https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1226251.pdf":"English Private Tutoring in Macao: Perceptions of Senior Secondary Three Students, V. Chan, ECNU Review of Education (2019)","https://www.gov.mo/en/news/120558/":"Macao-China: high-performing and fast-improving basic education system according to PISA 2018 (DSEJ, Macao SAR Government, 2019)"}}},"498":{"iso":"498","name":"Moldova","coverage":null,"summary":"Moldova tracks students into theoretical or vocational lyceum programs around age 14-15 and requires a national Bacalaureat exam to graduate.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Moldova splits upper-secondary students into theoretical (humanities/science) or vocational (arts, sports, theology, military) lyceum tracks.","Moldova requires a national Bacalaureat exam at the end of grade 12 to graduate; the U.S. has no equivalent.","One year of compulsory pre-primary education starts at age 5 in Moldova, earlier than most U.S. states' start of 5 to 6."],"common":["Like the U.S., local public authorities share responsibility for running compulsory schools alongside a national ministry that sets regulations."],"facts":{"start_age":"5","tracking_age":"14-15 (students enter theoretical or vocational lyceum tracks)","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education and Research)","exit_exam":"Bacalaureat"},"sources":[{"title":"Organisation of the Education System and its Structure - Eurydice Moldova","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/moldova/organisation-education-system-and-its-structure"},{"title":"Organisation of General Upper Secondary Education - Eurydice Moldova","url":"https://eurydice.eacea.ec.europa.eu/national-education-systems/moldova/organisation-general-upper-secondary-education"}],"_audit":{"supported":8,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"554":{"iso":"554","name":"New Zealand","coverage":null,"summary":"New Zealand's state and state-integrated schools have local autonomy under elected boards but share one national curriculum; private schools (4%) are exempt.","different":["Unlike the 50 U.S. states, one national curriculum and the NCEA qualification apply in New Zealand's state and state-integrated schools; private schools aren't required to follow it.","New Zealand offers M\u0101ori-medium education alongside English-medium schooling, a bicultural feature with no real U.S. parallel.","New Zealand's NCEA qualification is earned in stages (Levels 1-3) across the last three years of school, unlike single-sitting U.S. state tests."],"common":["Like the U.S., individual schools (run by elected boards) have considerable day-to-day operating autonomy.","Like the U.S., teachers must complete an approved tertiary teaching qualification and register with a national council before working in schools."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"none before 16","curriculum":"National (New Zealand Curriculum)","exit_exam":"NCEA (National Certificate of Educational Achievement)"},"sources":[{"title":"New Zealand - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/New-Zealand.pdf"}]},"600":{"iso":"600","name":"Paraguay","coverage":null,"summary":"Paraguay has no national school-leaving exam, like the U.S., but mandates bilingual Guaran\u00ed-Spanish instruction nationwide by law.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Paraguay teaches students in Guaran\u00ed, Spanish, or both as mother tongues under one national curriculum.","Paraguay splits upper-secondary students into scientific or technical Bachillerato tracks, or job-focused programs, around age 15, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools.","A 2010 reform made 12 years of schooling compulsory and free in Paraguay's public schools, more than most U.S. states legally require."],"common":["Like the U.S., Paraguay has no standardized national exit exam; researchers found no evidence of any exam to complete secondary school."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education and Sciences)","exit_exam":"None found for secondary/baccalaureate completion; a separate aptitude test exists only for vocational 'professional formation' graduates entering higher education"},"sources":[{"title":"CES Factbook Education Systems: Paraguay","url":"https://ethz.ch/content/dam/ethz/special-interest/mtec/edusys-dam/documents/CES%20Factbook%20Education%20Systems_Paraguay%20(1).pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":6,"corrected":3,"deleted":0}},"634":{"iso":"634","name":"Qatar","coverage":null,"summary":"Qatar centrally runs schools and splits students into academic or vocational secondary tracks at age 15, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Qatar's Ministry sets curriculum for public and private schools, but community (international) schools may follow their home country's curriculum instead.","At age 15, Qatari students choose an academic (university-bound) or vocational (employment-bound) secondary pathway, unlike undivided U.S. high schools.","Qatar's 'community schools' for expatriates may follow their home country's curriculum instead of Qatar's national one, unlike uniform U.S. public schools."],"common":["Like the U.S., Qatar operates public and private K-12 schools side by side within the same overall system."],"facts":{"start_age":"6","tracking_age":"15","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education and Higher Education)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Qatar - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Qatar.pdf"}]},"688":{"iso":"688","name":"Serbia","coverage":null,"summary":"Serbia requires a high-stakes exam at the end of 8-year primary school that determines secondary school placement, unlike the U.S.","different":["Unlike the U.S., Serbian secondary education is not compulsory at all; only the 8-year primary stage is legally required.","Serbia's Grade 8 exam (math, mother tongue, plus one science/humanities subject) decides secondary school placement; the U.S. has no equivalent gate.","After primary school, Serbian students choose gymnasium (academic), vocational, or artistic secondary schools, unlike comprehensive U.S. high schools."],"common":["Like the U.S., school funding in Serbia is shared between the national government and local governments."],"facts":{"start_age":"6.5-7.5","tracking_age":"~15 (end of 8-year primary school; students enter Grade 1 at 6.5-7.5)","curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education)","exit_exam":null},"sources":[{"title":"Serbia - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Serbia.pdf"}],"_audit":{"supported":7,"corrected":1,"deleted":0}},"410":{"iso":"410","name":"South Korea","coverage":null,"v2":{"rows":{"curriculum":{"text":"The Ministry of Education develops the national curriculum and publishes and approves textbooks. The 2022 revision, fully in place in 2025, added a high-school credit system allowing more subject choice.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"Korean schools follow a national curriculum developed by the Ministry of Education. The curriculum is revised every five to 10 years, with the latest revision completed in 2022 and implemented fully in 2025."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"Major revisions to the new curriculum include increased time for the development of literacy skills in early grades and English language skills in upper elementary, as well as a new credit system in high schools to allow for more choice of subjects and courses."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Korea.pdf","quote":"The specific responsibilities of the MOE include the following: \u2022 planning and coordinating education policies \u2022 formulating policies that govern primary, secondary, and higher education institutions \u2022 publishing and approving textbooks"}]},"sorting":{"text":"Admission is by lottery, not entrance exams, for lower secondary schools and about 60% of upper secondary schools; a few selective high schools exist; about 25% are in vocational programs.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"Students in \u201cequalization zones\u201d\u2014generally all major metropolitan areas\u2014are admitted to school by lottery rather than through entrance exams. These zones cover all lower secondary schools and about 60 percent of upper secondary schools."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"Some parents have perceived these reforms as a lowering of standards, and a small set of highly selective autonomous and specialty upper secondary schools has developed partially in response."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"More than 75 percent of these students attend general upper secondary schools, with the remaining 25 percent attending vocational upper secondary schools or programs"}]},"exams":{"text":"The CSAT university entrance exam is highly competitive and pressure is intense, but by 2019 over 75% of students were admitted early on broader criteria, including school records.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"The CSAT is a highly competitive test that traditionally was the primary entry criterion for university. Many Korean students take classes outside of school in hagwons (tutoring centers) to prepare for the test and pressure to do well is intense."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"In 1997, Korea created an early admissions process that considers broader criteria including the student records, extra-curricular activities, and additional essay exams by universities alongside CSAT scores. This route gained steam in the 2010s and became the primary route for admission since then, with over 75 percent of students admitted this way by 2019."}]},"teachers":{"text":"Primary teacher trainees generally come from their cohort's top tier; public-school jobs require a rigorous employment exam, and only about one in five trained secondary teachers works as a teacher.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"Applicants accepted into primary school teacher education programs generally come from the top tier of their secondary school cohort, and all teachers must pass a rigorous employment exam in order to work in public schools."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"Due to the oversupply of secondary school teachers, candidates must score very well on the employment exam to qualify for a teaching spot, making hiring rather than school admission the key selection point. Indeed, only about one in five trained secondary school teachers works as teachers."}]},"tutoring":{"text":"Most students take paid private lessons, often at hagwons (tutoring centers). The official 'private education' participation rate was 75.7% in 2025, with total spending of 27.5 trillion won.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"As of 2023, almost 80 percent of Korean primary and secondary school students worked with private tutors, often at private after-school tutoring centers (hagwons)."},{"url":"https://mods.go.kr/boardDownload.es?bid=11758&list_no=444220&seq=1","quote":"The private education participation rate of elementary, middle and high school students stood at 75.7% in 2025, down 4.3%p from 2024."},{"url":"https://mods.go.kr/boardDownload.es?bid=11758&list_no=444220&seq=1","quote":"The total private education expenditures of elementary, middle and high school students recorded 27.5 trillion won in 2025, falling by 5.7% from 2024."}]}},"borrow":[{"text":"NCEE says teacher quality contributes significantly to Korea's success, pointing to four-year teacher-preparation colleges, forecasting of teacher supply and demand, higher salaries and legal job protections.","cites":[{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"Teacher quality contributes significantly to Korean success. In response to severe secondary school teacher shortages in the 1960s and 1970s, Korea took steps toward building a strong, highly qualified teaching force."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"These included establishing four-year colleges and universities dedicated to teacher preparation; developing a system for forecasting and responding to teacher supply and demand; and increasing the salaries of teachers."},{"url":"https://ncee.org/country/korea/","quote":"The government also legally guaranteed teachers job protections, competitive salaries, and authority and input into educational matters."}]},{"text":"OECD analysts (2018) find disadvantaged Korean students at least as likely as advantaged ones to have high-quality teachers; teachers rotate schools every five years, with extra pay and promotion credit for high-need postings.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2018/06/effective-teacher-policies_g1g8f550/9789264301603-en.pdf","quote":"This report shows that socio-economically disadvantaged students in Japan and Korea are at least as likely as advantaged students to be taught by high-quality teachers, as measured by characteristics such as years of experience, being certified for all the subjects taught"},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2018/06/effective-teacher-policies_g1g8f550/9789264301603-en.pdf","quote":"A mandatory rotation scheme for teachers in Korea means that teachers are required to move to a different school every five years. Within this scheme, multiple incentives are offered to attract teachers to high-needs schools, including additional salary, smaller classes, less instructional time, additional credit towards future promotion to administrative positions"}]}],"tradeoffs":[{"text":"Korea's fertility rate, 0.72 in 2023, is the world's lowest. The OECD (2025) says fierce competition for prestigious universities and high private-education spending magnify the costs of having children; 49.2% of parents find tutoring costs very burdensome (2022).","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/korea-s-unborn-future_1b836111/005ce8f7-en.pdf","quote":"Korea is facing an unprecedented demographic challenge, with the lowest fertility rate in the world. In 2023, the country\u2019s total fertility rate was down to 0.72"},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/korea-s-unborn-future_1b836111/005ce8f7-en.pdf","quote":"In Korea, the direct costs of having children are magnified compared to many OECD countries by fierce competition to enter prestigious universities and high private education expenditure (credentialism)."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/korea-s-unborn-future_1b836111/005ce8f7-en.pdf","quote":"Indeed, 49.2% of Korean parents think the cost of private tutoring is very burdensome (KEDI, 2022), and 27% point to the burden of their children's education and childcare as one of the main reasons for the falling fertility rate (PCASPP, 2023)."}]},{"text":"The OECD (2025) notes intensive tutoring harms adolescent mental health and Korean youth report low life satisfaction; in a 2017 study, more than 80% of Korean college students saw high school as a 'life-or-death battlefield'.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/korea-s-unborn-future_1b836111/005ce8f7-en.pdf","quote":"Intensive tutoring has a detrimental effect on adolescent mental health (Xu and Lee, 2023), and Korean youth report low life satisfaction relative to other OECD countries (OECD, 2023f)."},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/korea-s-unborn-future_1b836111/005ce8f7-en.pdf","quote":"More than 80% of Korean college students perceive high school as a \u201clife-or-death battlefield\u201d, a significantly higher proportion than in the United States (40%), China (42%) and Japan (14%), countries with relatively comparable entrance exam systems (Kim, 2017)."}]}],"sources":{"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Korea.pdf":"TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia: Republic of Korea (Korea Institute for Curriculum and Evaluation, 2024)","https://ncee.org/country/korea/":"Korea country profile (NCEE, updated 2025)","https://mods.go.kr/boardDownload.es?bid=11758&list_no=444220&seq=1":"Private Education Expenditures Survey of Elementary, Middle and High School Students in 2025 (Ministry of Data and Statistics and Ministry of Education, 2026)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2018/06/effective-teacher-policies_g1g8f550/9789264301603-en.pdf":"Effective Teacher Policies: Insights from PISA (OECD, 2018)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/korea-s-unborn-future_1b836111/005ce8f7-en.pdf":"Korea's Unborn Future: Understanding Low-Fertility Trends (OECD, 2025)"}}},"762":{"iso":"762","name":"Tajikistan (Dushanbe)","coverage":"Scores cover Dushanbe, the capital city, only, and may not represent the rest of Tajikistan.","summary":"Tajikistan runs one national school system; a 2024 law extended compulsory schooling through grade 11 (11 years total).","different":["Unlike the U.S., Tajikistan's national Ministry of Education and Science sets one curriculum and standard for the entire country.","Tajikistan extended compulsory schooling from 9 to 11 years in 2024, now covering ages 7 to 18; the U.S. requires schooling only to about 16-18."],"common":["Like the U.S., Tajikistan structures basic education as primary school followed by secondary stages before higher education, similar to elementary and secondary school."],"facts":{"start_age":"7","tracking_age":null,"curriculum":"National (Ministry of Education and Science)","exit_exam":"Certificate of general secondary education, awarded after completing grade 11 under the 2024 law"},"sources":[{"title":"Tajikistan country overview - UNESCO HerAtlas","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/heratlas/country-overview?country=TJ"},{"title":"Ministry of Education and Science of the Republic of Tajikistan - official site","url":"https://maorif.tj/"}],"_audit":{"supported":9,"corrected":0,"deleted":0}},"826":{"iso":"826","name":"United Kingdom (England)","coverage":"TIMSS, PIRLS and PIAAC results cover England only; PISA 2025 covers the whole United Kingdom. This note describes England's system; Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland run their own.","summary":"England mixes local-authority schools, which must follow the National Curriculum, with academies and free schools, only expected to match its breadth.","different":["Unlike the U.S.'s 50 state standards, England applies one National Curriculum, though many academies have flexibility to depart from it.","England gives national GCSE exams at age 16, externally set and graded; the U.S. has no equivalent national exit exam."],"common":["Like the U.S. requiring a teaching license, English teachers need Qualified Teacher Status (QTS), a formal certification process."],"facts":{"start_age":"5","tracking_age":"11 (transition from primary to secondary school; source does not describe selective/ability-based admission)","curriculum":"National Curriculum (state-funded schools)","exit_exam":"GCSEs (age 16): externally set and graded, on a 9-1 scale"},"sources":[{"title":"England - TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia","url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/England.pdf"}]},"840":{"iso":"840","name":"United States","coverage":null,"v2":{"rows":{"curriculum":{"text":"States and communities develop curricula: states typically set standards and a curricular framework that local districts customize. In 2020, about 35 states used Common Core math standards, fully or partially.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.ed.gov/about/ed-overview/federal-role-in-education","quote":"Education is primarily a State and local responsibility in the United States. It is States and communities, as well as public and private organizations of all kinds, that establish schools and colleges, develop curricula, and determine requirements for enrollment and graduation."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/United-States.pdf","quote":"States have autonomy in shaping academic standards, selecting student assessment instruments, and establishing teacher licensure and certification requirements. Typically, states establish a statewide curricular framework and allow local school districts to customize their curricula."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/United-States.pdf","quote":"As of 2020, an estimated 35 states, the District of Columbia, and DoDEA fully or partially use the CCSS in mathematics for their state"}]},"sorting":{"text":"Secondary schools have long been comprehensive and students are generally not formally streamed, but informal tracking within schools is common, especially in high school (e.g., honors and Advanced Placement courses).","cites":[{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"a country whose secondary schools have been organised as comprehensive schools for a long time, and whose elementary schools are open to all"},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"Students are generally not formally streamed, schools are not selective in their admissions process, and students usually do not repeat grades"},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf","quote":"there is a high prevalence of informal streaming and tracking within schools in the United States, often starting in the early grades and particularly so in high schools (e.g. Advanced Placement courses, honours courses, etc.)"}]},"exams":{"text":"Federal law (ESSA) requires yearly state reading and math tests in grades 3\u20138 and once in high school. College-admission tests were optional at 89% of Common App colleges in 2021.","cites":[{"url":"https://www.ed.gov/sites/ed/files/policy/elsec/leg/essa/essaassessmentfactsheet1207.pdf","quote":"the law maintains the requirement that states administer to all students annual statewide assessments in reading/language arts and mathematics in grades 3-8 and once in high school, as well as assessments once in each grade span in science for all students"},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/United-States.pdf","quote":"In 2021, 89% of institutions participating in the online common application system made standardized testing optional for postsecondary admissions."}]},"teachers":{"text":"States set licensure and certification rules and often require ongoing training to renew licenses. About 18% of public school teachers (2015\u201316) entered through alternative certification routes.","cites":[{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/United-States.pdf","quote":"States have autonomy in shaping academic standards, selecting student assessment instruments, and establishing teacher licensure and certification requirements."},{"url":"https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/United-States.pdf","quote":"Individual states have the authority to determine professional development requirements for practicing mathematics and science teachers in Grades 4 and 8. States often require continuing professional development to renew teacher licenses."},{"url":"https://nces.ed.gov/programs/coe/indicator/tlc","quote":"Approximately 18 percent of public school teachers in 2015\u201316 had entered teaching through an alternative route to certification program."}]},"tutoring":{"text":null,"cites":[],"why_missing":"No solid, recent nationally representative figure on how many U.S. students use paid private tutoring was found in NCES, U.S. Department of Education, TIMSS 2023 or OECD sources."}},"borrow":[],"tradeoffs":[],"sources":{"https://www.ed.gov/about/ed-overview/federal-role-in-education":"Federal Role in Education (U.S. Department of Education)","https://timss2023.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/United-States.pdf":"TIMSS 2023 Encyclopedia: United States (IEA, 2024)","https://www.ed.gov/sites/ed/files/policy/elsec/leg/essa/essaassessmentfactsheet1207.pdf":"Every Student Succeeds Act: assessment regulations summary (U.S. Department of Education, 2016)","https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2011/05/lessons-from-pisa-for-the-united-states_g1g123da/9789264096660-en.pdf":"Strong Performers and Successful Reformers in Education: Lessons from PISA for the United States (OECD, 2011)","https://nces.ed.gov/programs/coe/indicator/tlc":"Characteristics of Public School Teachers Who Completed Alternative Route to Certification Programs (NCES Condition of Education, 2018)"}}}};